window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-chem-solutions","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Solutions · Practice","chapter":"Solutions","subject":"Chemistry","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":1,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (binary liquid mixture) with List-II (behaviour).\nList-I:\n(A) n-Hexane + n-heptane\n(B) Ethanol + acetone\n(C) Chloroform + acetone\n(D) Nitric acid + water (about 68% acid by mass)\nList-II:\n(I) Positive deviation caused by breaking of hydrogen bonds\n(II) Maximum boiling azeotrope boiling at 393.5 K\n(III) Nearly ideal, $\\Delta_{mix}H \\approx 0$ and $\\Delta_{mix}V \\approx 0$\n(IV) Negative deviation caused by new hydrogen bonds between unlike molecules","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) n-Hexane and n-heptane have very similar A–A, B–B and A–B forces, so the mixture is nearly ideal (III).\n(B) Acetone molecules break some of the hydrogen bonds in ethanol. This raises the vapour pressure, giving a positive deviation (I).\n(C) The H of chloroform hydrogen-bonds to the O of acetone. This lowers the vapour pressure, giving a negative deviation (IV).\n(D) The 68% HNO$_3$–water mixture is a maximum boiling azeotrope with b.p. 393.5 K (II).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":2,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"6.0 g of a non-volatile, non-electrolyte solute is dissolved in 180 g of water (molar mass 18 g mol$^{-1}$). The vapour pressure of pure water at this temperature is 32.00 mm Hg, and that of the solution is 31.68 mm Hg. Using the dilute-solution approximation, what is the molar mass of the solute?","image":"","options":["60 g mol$^{-1}$","120 g mol$^{-1}$","30 g mol$^{-1}$","600 g mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $w_2$ = 6.0 g, $w_1$ = 180 g, $M_1$ = 18 g mol$^{-1}$, $\\Delta p$ = 32.00 − 31.68 = 0.32 mm Hg.\nFormula: $\\frac{\\Delta p}{p_1^0} = \\frac{w_2 M_1}{M_2 w_1}$, which rearranges to $M_2 = \\frac{w_2 M_1 p_1^0}{w_1 \\Delta p}$.\nSubstituting: $M_2 = \\frac{6.0 \\times 18 \\times 32.00}{180 \\times 0.32}$.\nSo $M_2$ = 60 g mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":3,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which statement best explains why adding a non-volatile solute to a solvent lowers the vapour pressure?","image":"","options":["Solute particles take up part of the liquid surface, so fewer solvent molecules can escape from the surface","Solute particles react chemically with solvent molecules and form a less volatile compound","The solute raises the boiling point, which then lowers the vapour pressure","Solute particles evaporate and oppose the escape of solvent vapour"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In a pure liquid, solvent molecules cover the whole surface.\nIn a solution, solute particles also occupy part of the surface, so the fraction of surface covered by solvent molecules is smaller.\nFewer solvent molecules escape per unit time, so the vapour pressure falls.\nOption (3) reverses cause and effect: the lowering of vapour pressure is what causes the boiling point to rise.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":4,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Henry's law constant for N$_2$ in water is 76.48 kbar at 293 K and 88.84 kbar at 303 K. Water is first equilibrated with N$_2$ at partial pressure $p$ at 293 K. In a second experiment, the partial pressure of N$_2$ is doubled and the temperature is raised to 303 K. What is the ratio of the mole fraction of dissolved N$_2$ in the second experiment to that in the first?","image":"","options":["1.72","2.32","2.00","0.86"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $K_H(293) = 76.48$ kbar, $K_H(303) = 88.84$ kbar.\nFormula: $x = \\frac{p}{K_H}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{x_2}{x_1} = \\frac{2p / 88.84}{p / 76.48} = \\frac{2 \\times 76.48}{88.84}$.\nSo the ratio $= 1.72$.\nDoubling the pressure doubles the solubility, but the higher $K_H$ at 303 K partly offsets this, so the ratio is less than 2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":5,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"The air in scuba divers' tanks is diluted with helium (about 11.7% He, 56.2% N$_2$ and 32.1% O$_2$). What is the main purpose of this dilution?","image":"","options":["To increase the solubility of oxygen in blood at great depths","To avoid bends and the toxic effects of a high concentration of nitrogen in blood","To make the gas mixture denser so that divers can sink easily","To increase the partial pressure of nitrogen so that more of it dissolves in blood"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At depth, the high pressure raises the solubility of the inhaled gases in blood (Henry's law).\nOn surfacing, the dissolved nitrogen comes out as bubbles. These bubbles block capillaries and cause bends.\nDiluting the air with helium lowers the amount of nitrogen that dissolves. Helium itself has a very high $K_H$, so it is poorly soluble.\nOption (4) is the opposite of what is wanted.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":6,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Osmotic pressure is preferred for finding molar masses of proteins partly because it is measured around room temperature.\n(ii) Red blood cells placed in 0.5% (mass/volume) NaCl solution shrink.\n(iii) A cellulose acetate membrane is permeable to water but impermeable to the ions present in sea water.\n(iv) When two isotonic solutions are separated by a semipermeable membrane, solvent flows from the one with the lower molar mass solute to the other.\n(v) The unit of the ebullioscopic constant $K_b$ is K kg $mol^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: biomolecules are generally not stable at higher temperatures, so a room-temperature measurement suits them.\n(ii) Incorrect: 0.5% NaCl is hypotonic compared with 0.9% saline, so water enters the cells and they swell.\n(iii) Correct: this property is why cellulose acetate is used for reverse osmosis.\n(iv) Incorrect: isotonic solutions have the same osmotic pressure, so no osmosis occurs between them.\n(v) Correct.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":7,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"On the basis of solute–solvent interactions, which of the following gives the correct order of increasing solubility in water?","image":"","options":["Ethanol < diethyl ether < n-hexane","n-Hexane < ethanol < diethyl ether","Diethyl ether < n-hexane < ethanol","n-Hexane < diethyl ether < ethanol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Water is a polar, strongly hydrogen-bonded solvent.\nn-Hexane is non-polar and cannot interact favourably with water, so it is the least soluble.\nDiethyl ether is moderately polar. Its oxygen can only accept hydrogen bonds from water, and its alkyl part is large, so it is only partly soluble.\nEthanol both donates and accepts hydrogen bonds with water, which makes it the most soluble.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":8,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Increasing the external pressure has no significant effect on the solubility of a solid in a liquid. The reason is:","image":"","options":["Solids and liquids are highly incompressible","Dissolution of every solid is endothermic","Solids have zero vapour pressure","Pressure affects only ionic solutes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, pressure does not significantly affect the solubility of solids in liquids.\nSolids and liquids are highly incompressible, so they remain practically unaffected by pressure changes.\nOption (2) is false: the sign of $\\Delta_{sol}H$ varies from solute to solute.\nOptions (3) and (4) are not the reason given in the chapter.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":9,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The osmotic pressure method is preferred for determining the molar masses of proteins and polymers.\nStatement II: Osmotic pressure is measured at around room temperature, and its magnitude is large even for very dilute solutions.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT says the osmotic pressure method is widely used for proteins, polymers and other macromolecules.\nStatement II is correct: the measurement is done near room temperature, which suits biomolecules that are unstable at high temperature.\nIts magnitude is also large even in dilute solutions, which suits polymers of poor solubility.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":10,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following concentration terms do NOT change when the temperature of a solution is changed?\nMass percentage (w/w), mole fraction, molality, molarity, mass by volume percentage (w/V), parts per million (mass to mass)","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Terms based only on masses or moles are independent of temperature, because mass does not change with temperature.\nThese are mass %, mole fraction, molality and ppm (mass to mass), which makes 4 terms.\nMolarity and w/V % both use the volume of the solution, and volume changes with temperature.\nSo exactly 4 of the listed terms are temperature independent.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":11,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"1.5 g of ethanoic acid ($CH_3COOH$, molar mass 60 g mol$^{-1}$) is dissolved in 50 g of benzene. The freezing point is lowered by 1.408 K. $K_f$ for benzene is 5.12 K kg mol$^{-1}$. If the acid dimerises in benzene, what is its percentage association?","image":"","options":["45%","55%","90%","80%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: n = 1.5/60 = 0.025 mol and m = 0.025/0.050 = 0.5 mol kg$^{-1}$.\nCalculated $\\Delta T_f = K_f m$ = 5.12 × 0.5 = 2.56 K.\nThe van't Hoff factor is i = observed/calculated = 1.408/2.56 = 0.55.\nFor dimerisation, i = 1 − x/2. So x/2 = 0.45 and x = 0.90.\nSo the percentage association is 90%. Option (2), 55%, is the value of i expressed as a percentage, not x.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":12,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"What is the mole fraction of urea in a 2 molal aqueous urea solution? (molar mass of water = 18 g mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["0.0360","0.0200","0.0347","0.0694"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 2 m means 2 mol of urea in 1000 g of water.\nMoles of water = 1000/18 = 55.56 mol\nFormula: x$_{urea}$ = n$_{urea}$/(n$_{urea}$ + n$_{water}$)\nSubstituting: x = 2/(2 + 55.56) = 2/57.56\nSo x$_{urea}$ = 0.0347. The value 0.0360 comes from wrongly leaving the solute moles out of the denominator.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":13,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Estimate the molal elevation constant of water using $K_b = \\frac{R M_1 T_b^2}{1000\\,\\Delta_{vap}H}$.\nData: R = 8.314 J $mol^{-1}$ $K^{-1}$, $M_1$ = 18 g $mol^{-1}$, $T_b$ = 373.15 K, $\\Delta_{vap}H$ = 40.66 kJ $mol^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["0.512 K kg $mol^{-1}$","512 K kg $mol^{-1}$","1.86 K kg $mol^{-1}$","0.0512 K kg $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R = 8.314 J $mol^{-1}$ $K^{-1}$, $M_1$ = 18 g $mol^{-1}$, $T_b$ = 373.15 K, $\\Delta_{vap}H$ = 40660 J $mol^{-1}$ (convert kJ to J).\nFormula: $K_b = \\frac{R M_1 T_b^2}{1000\\,\\Delta_{vap}H}$.\nSubstituting: $K_b$ = (8.314 × 18 × 139241)/(1000 × 40660) ≈ 2.084 × $10^{7}$ / 4.066 × $10^{7}$.\nSo $K_b$ ≈ 0.512 K kg $mol^{-1}$, which agrees with Table 1.3 (0.52).\nOption (2) results from leaving $\\Delta_{vap}H$ in kJ.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":14,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Liquids A and B form an ideal solution. At a certain temperature, the vapour pressure of pure A is 300 mm Hg and that of pure B is 100 mm Hg. The mole fraction of A in the liquid is 0.25. What is the mole fraction of A in the vapour in equilibrium with this liquid?","image":"","options":["0.25","0.50","0.75","0.33"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $p_A^0$ = 300 mm Hg, $p_B^0$ = 100 mm Hg, $x_A$ = 0.25, so $x_B$ = 0.75.\nFormula: $p_i = x_i p_i^0$ and $y_A = p_A/p_{total}$.\nSubstituting: $p_A$ = 0.25 × 300 = 75 mm Hg and $p_B$ = 0.75 × 100 = 75 mm Hg, so $p_{total}$ = 150 mm Hg.\nSo $y_A$ = 75/150 = 0.50. The vapour is richer in A, the more volatile component, than the liquid is.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":15,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Climbers at high altitudes often become weak and unable to think clearly, a condition called anoxia. According to Henry's law, the root cause is:","image":"","options":["Lower partial pressure of oxygen in the air, which lowers the concentration of oxygen in the blood","Higher partial pressure of nitrogen at altitude, which forms nitrogen bubbles in the blood","Increased solubility of oxygen in blood at the low temperatures of high altitudes","A larger Henry's law constant for oxygen caused by the lower air pressure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At high altitude, the partial pressure of $O_2$ is lower than at ground level.\nBy Henry's law, the amount of a gas dissolved is proportional to its partial pressure. So less $O_2$ dissolves in the blood and tissues, and the result is anoxia.\nNitrogen bubbles in the blood cause the bends in divers, not anoxia.\n$K_H$ depends on the gas and the temperature, not on the external pressure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":16,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"2.56 g of a non-volatile, non-electrolyte solid is dissolved in 100 g of cyclohexane. The freezing point of the solvent is lowered by 4.0 K. Given $K_f$ for cyclohexane = 20.0 K kg mol$^{-1}$, what is the molar mass of the solid?","image":"","options":["128 g mol$^{-1}$","64 g mol$^{-1}$","256 g mol$^{-1}$","12.8 g mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $w_2$ = 2.56 g, $w_1$ = 100 g, $\\Delta T_f$ = 4.0 K, $K_f$ = 20.0 K kg mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $M_2 = \\frac{K_f \\times w_2 \\times 1000}{\\Delta T_f \\times w_1}$.\nSubstituting: $M_2 = \\frac{20.0 \\times 2.56 \\times 1000}{4.0 \\times 100}$.\nThis gives $M_2$ = 51200/400 = 128 g mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":17,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (phenomenon) with List-II (explanation based on gas solubility):\nList-I\n(A) Soft drink bottles are sealed under high pressure\n(B) Bends in scuba divers\n(C) Anoxia in climbers at high altitude\n(D) Aquatic life prefers cold water\nList-II\n(I) Low partial pressure of oxygen in air\n(II) Solubility of a gas increases with its partial pressure\n(III) Dissolved nitrogen escapes as bubbles when pressure decreases\n(IV) Solubility of a gas increases as temperature falls","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) A high CO$_2$ pressure raises the solubility of CO$_2$ in the drink, so A matches II.\n(B) As divers come up, the pressure falls and dissolved N$_2$ forms bubbles in blood, so B matches III.\n(C) At altitude the partial pressure of O$_2$ is low, so blood oxygen is low, so C matches I.\n(D) Gas dissolution is exothermic, so O$_2$ is more soluble in cold water, so D matches IV.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":18,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (binary mixture) with List-II (characteristic feature).\nList-I:\n(A) Ethanol + acetone\n(B) n-Hexane + n-heptane\n(C) Nitric acid + water\n(D) Chloroform + acetone\nList-II:\n(P) $\\Delta_{mix}H \\approx 0$ and $\\Delta_{mix}V \\approx 0$\n(Q) Hydrogen bond forms between the C–H of one component and the C=O oxygen of the other\n(R) Added molecules break some hydrogen bonds of the host liquid, raising the vapour pressure\n(S) Maximum boiling azeotrope at about 68% of one component by mass","image":"","options":["A-Q, B-P, C-S, D-R","A-R, B-P, C-S, D-Q","A-R, B-S, C-P, D-Q","A-P, B-R, C-Q, D-S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-R: acetone breaks the hydrogen bonds of ethanol, giving positive deviation.\nB-P: n-hexane and n-heptane form a nearly ideal solution, with no heat or volume change on mixing.\nC-S: nitric acid and water form a maximum boiling azeotrope (about 68% $HNO_3$ by mass, boiling at 393.5 K).\nD-Q: chloroform's C–H forms a hydrogen bond with acetone's carbonyl oxygen, giving negative deviation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":19,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For an ideal solution of chloroform and dichloromethane, the vapour in equilibrium with the solution is richer in dichloromethane, which is the more volatile component.\nReason (R): In an ideal solution, the mole fraction of each component in the vapour phase is equal to its mole fraction in the liquid phase.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. In the NCERT example, $x_{CH_2Cl_2}$ = 0.688 in the liquid, but $y_{CH_2Cl_2}$ = 0.82 in the vapour. The vapour is enriched in the more volatile component.\nR is false. Since $y_i = x_i p_i^0 / p_{total}$, the vapour composition equals the liquid composition only if $p_1^0 = p_2^0$ (or for an azeotrope). In general it is different.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":20,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Two liquids P and Q form an ideal solution. At 320 K, the vapour pressures of pure P and pure Q are 600 mm Hg and 200 mm Hg respectively. The vapour in equilibrium with a certain liquid mixture of P and Q is found to contain P at a mole fraction of 0.75. What is the total vapour pressure over this liquid mixture?","image":"","options":["300 mm Hg","350 mm Hg","400 mm Hg","500 mm Hg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $p_P^0$ = 600 mm Hg, $p_Q^0$ = 200 mm Hg, $y_P$ = 0.75.\nFormula: $y_P = \\frac{x_P p_P^0}{x_P p_P^0 + (1 - x_P) p_Q^0}$.\nSubstituting: $0.75 = \\frac{600 x_P}{400 x_P + 200}$, so $300 x_P = 150$ and $x_P$ = 0.5.\nThen $p_{total}$ = 200 + (600 − 200) × 0.5 = 400 mm Hg.\nThe tempting value 500 mm Hg comes from wrongly weighting by the vapour fractions: 0.75 × 600 + 0.25 × 200.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":21,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a solid whose dissolution in a nearly saturated solution is endothermic ($\\Delta_{sol}H > 0$), solubility increases with rise in temperature.\nReason (R): The equilibrium Solute + Solvent $\\rightleftharpoons$ Solution is dynamic and obeys Le Chatelier's principle.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: the dissolution–crystallisation equilibrium is dynamic, so Le Chatelier's principle applies.\nA is true: when $\\Delta_{sol}H > 0$, heat behaves like a reactant. Raising the temperature therefore shifts the equilibrium towards the solution, and more solid dissolves.\nThis is exactly the reasoning behind A, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":22,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Aquatic species are more comfortable in cold water than in warm water. Which of the following is the correct reason?","image":"","options":["$K_H$ of oxygen is higher at lower temperature, so more oxygen dissolves","$K_H$ of oxygen is lower at lower temperature, so oxygen is more soluble in cold water","The partial pressure of oxygen above water is higher at lower temperature","Dissolution of oxygen in water is endothermic, so it is favoured at low temperature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$K_H$ of O$_2$ rises from 34.86 kbar at 293 K to 46.82 kbar at 303 K.\nSince $x = \\frac{p}{K_H}$, a lower $K_H$ at lower temperature means greater solubility.\nCold water therefore holds more dissolved oxygen.\nDissolving a gas is exothermic, not endothermic, so option (4) is wrong. Option (1) reverses the trend in $K_H$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":23,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"For a binary liquid mixture at constant temperature, a plot of total vapour pressure against mole fraction of component 2 is a curve that rises above the straight Raoult's law line. The curve passes through a maximum at an intermediate composition, where the vapour pressure is higher than that of either pure liquid. Which inference about this mixture is correct?","image":"","options":["The components show negative deviation, and the mixture at the maximum composition boils at the highest temperature","The mixture contracts on mixing, and fractional distillation can separate it into both pure components","Heat is absorbed on mixing, and the mixture at the maximum composition distils at constant temperature with the lowest boiling point","A-B interactions are stronger than A-A and B-B interactions, as in phenol-aniline"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Vapour pressure higher than the Raoult's law value means positive deviation. Here A-B attractions are weaker than A-A and B-B, so $\\Delta_{mix}H > 0$ (heat is absorbed) and $\\Delta_{mix}V > 0$.\nA solution boils when its vapour pressure equals the external pressure. The composition with maximum vapour pressure therefore boils at the lowest temperature.\nThat composition is a minimum boiling azeotrope, which distils unchanged at constant temperature.\nOptions (1) and (4) describe negative deviation. Option (2) fails because the volume expands and the azeotrope prevents complete separation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":24,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"0.01 mol of a weak monobasic acid HA is dissolved in 1 kg of water. The observed depression in freezing point is 0.02232 K. $K_f$ for water is 1.86 K kg mol$^{-1}$. The acid dissociates as HA ⇌ $H^+$ + $A^-$. Treating molality as equal to molarity for this dilute solution, what is the dissociation constant $K_a$ of HA?","image":"","options":["$5.0 \\times 10^{-4}$","$4.0 \\times 10^{-4}$","$2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$","$2.5 \\times 10^{-4}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.01 mol/kg. The calculated $\\Delta T_f$ (no dissociation) is $1.86 \\times 0.01$ = 0.0186 K.\nvan't Hoff factor: i = observed/calculated = 0.02232/0.0186 = 1.2.\nFor HA, i = 1 + α, so α = 0.2.\nFormula: $K_a = \\frac{c\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha} = \\frac{0.01 \\times 0.04}{0.8}$.\nSo $K_a = 5.0 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nThe value $4.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ wrongly ignores the (1 − α) term.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":25,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Dissolution of a gas in a liquid is similar to condensation and releases heat, so the solubility of the gas decreases with rise in temperature.\nStatement II: The Henry's law constant of N$_2$ in water is smaller at 303 K than at 293 K.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Gas dissolution is exothermic, so by Le Chatelier's principle a rise in temperature lowers the solubility.\nStatement II is incorrect. $K_H$ of N$_2$ is 76.48 kbar at 293 K and 88.84 kbar at 303 K, so it increases with temperature.\nA larger $K_H$ means lower solubility, which is consistent with Statement I.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":26,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"The vapour pressure of pure water at 298 K is 3.17 kPa. What is the vapour pressure of water over a 2.0 molal aqueous solution of a non-volatile non-electrolyte at 298 K? (Molar mass of water = 18 g $mol^{-1}$; do not use the dilute-solution approximation.)","image":"","options":["3.06 kPa","2.96 kPa","0.11 kPa","3.28 kPa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p_1^0$ = 3.17 kPa. A 2.0 m solution has $n_2$ = 2.0 mol in 1000 g of water, so $n_1 = \\frac{1000}{18}$ = 55.56 mol.\nFormula: $p_1 = x_1 p_1^0$, where $x_1 = \\frac{n_1}{n_1+n_2}$.\nSubstituting: $x_1 = \\frac{55.56}{57.56}$ = 0.9653, so $p_1 = 0.9653 \\times 3.17$ = 3.06 kPa.\nWhy the others are wrong: 0.11 kPa is only the lowering $\\Delta p_1$. 3.28 kPa wrongly adds the lowering to $p_1^0$. 2.96 kPa wrongly doubles the solute particles, which applies only to an electrolyte that splits into two ions.\nSo the vapour pressure of the solution is 3.06 kPa.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":27,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A solution of hydrogen in palladium is classified as which type of solution?","image":"","options":["Gaseous solution with a solid solute","Liquid solution with a gaseous solute","Solid solution with a gaseous solute","Solid solution with a liquid solute"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Hydrogen is a gas and acts as the solute. Palladium is a solid and acts as the solvent.\nThe solvent decides the physical state of the solution, so this is a solid solution with a gaseous solute (NCERT Table 1.1).\nOption (1) wrongly treats the gas as the solvent.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":28,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A binary liquid mixture distils at a constant temperature of 393.5 K with the composition of about 68% of one component and 32% water by mass. This mixture is an example of a:","image":"","options":["Minimum boiling azeotrope formed by ethanol and water","Minimum boiling azeotrope formed by nitric acid and water","Maximum boiling azeotrope formed by nitric acid and water","Maximum boiling azeotrope formed by ethanol and water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Solutions with a large negative deviation from Raoult's law form maximum boiling azeotropes.\nNCERT's example is nitric acid–water, with about 68% $HNO_3$ and 32% water by mass, boiling at 393.5 K.\nEthanol–water shows a large positive deviation. It gives a minimum boiling azeotrope at about 95% ethanol by volume, so options (1) and (4) are wrong.\nOption (2) names the correct liquids but the wrong type of azeotrope.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":29,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"According to Raoult's law for a solution of two volatile liquids, the partial vapour pressure of each component is directly proportional to its:","image":"","options":["mole fraction in the vapour phase","mole fraction in the liquid solution","molality in the solution","molarity in the solution"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Raoult's law: for a solution of volatile liquids, $p_i = p_i^0 x_i$, where $x_i$ is the mole fraction of component i in the solution (liquid).\nThe vapour-phase mole fraction $y_i$ appears in Dalton's law, $p_i = y_i p_{total}$, not in Raoult's law.\nMolality and molarity are not used in this form of the law.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":30,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Normal saline is 0.9% (mass/volume) NaCl and is isotonic with the fluid in blood cells. Assume NaCl is completely dissociated (i = 2). What % (mass/volume) aqueous glucose solution would be isotonic with normal saline at the same temperature? (Molar masses: NaCl = 58.5 g mol$^{-1}$, glucose = 180 g mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["5.54%","2.77%","0.90%","11.08%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 0.9% (m/V) NaCl means 9 g per litre, so C = 9/58.5 = 0.1538 M.\nFormula: isotonic solutions need equal $iC$ at the same T, since $\\Pi = iCRT$.\nFor NaCl, $iC = 2 \\times 0.1538 = 0.3077$ M of particles.\nGlucose has i = 1, so it needs 0.3077 M, which is $0.3077 \\times 180 = 55.4$ g L$^{-1}$.\nSo the glucose solution must be 5.54% (m/V).\nIgnoring dissociation would wrongly give 2.77%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":31,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (solvent) with List-II (molal freezing point depression constant, $K_f$ in K kg $mol^{-1}$) as given in NCERT Table 1.3.\nList-I:\n(A) Water\n(B) Benzene\n(C) Cyclohexane\n(D) Acetic acid\nList-II:\n(I) 20.00\n(II) 3.90\n(III) 1.86\n(IV) 5.12","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Water: $K_f$ = 1.86, so A-III.\nBenzene: $K_f$ = 5.12, so B-IV.\nCyclohexane: $K_f$ = 20.00, so C-I.\nAcetic acid: $K_f$ = 3.90, so D-II.\nOption (2) swaps the values for benzene and acetic acid.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":32,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"In a plot of vapour pressure against temperature, curves are drawn for pure water and for an aqueous sucrose solution. A horizontal line is drawn at 1.013 bar. Which description of this diagram is correct?","image":"","options":["The solution curve lies below the pure-water curve and meets the 1.013 bar line at a temperature above 373.15 K","The solution curve lies above the pure-water curve and meets the 1.013 bar line at a temperature below 373.15 K","The two curves coincide because sucrose is non-volatile","The solution curve lies below the pure-water curve and meets the 1.013 bar line at a temperature below 373.15 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A non-volatile solute lowers the vapour pressure of the solvent at every temperature, so the solution curve lies below the pure-water curve.\nA liquid boils when its vapour pressure equals the external pressure (1.013 bar).\nThe lower curve therefore reaches 1.013 bar only at a higher temperature. This is the elevation of boiling point, $\\Delta T_b$.\nOption (4) places the lower curve correctly but reverses the direction of the boiling-point shift.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":33,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Red blood cells are placed in a 0.5% (mass/volume) NaCl solution. What happens to the cells?","image":"","options":["They shrink, because the solution is hypertonic","They swell, because the solution is hypotonic","No change occurs, because the solution is isotonic","They shrink, because the solution is hypotonic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The fluid inside blood cells is isotonic with 0.9% (m/V) NaCl, which is normal saline.\nA 0.5% NaCl solution has a lower salt concentration, so it is hypotonic.\nWater therefore flows into the cells by osmosis, and they swell.\nShrinking would happen only in a hypertonic solution, that is, more than 0.9% NaCl.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":34,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (example) with List-II (type of solution, written as solute in solvent).\nList-I\n(A) Camphor in nitrogen gas\n(B) Hydrogen in palladium\n(C) Amalgam of mercury with sodium\n(D) Chloroform mixed with nitrogen gas\nList-II\n(I) Solid in gas\n(II) Gas in solid\n(III) Liquid in solid\n(IV) Liquid in gas","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Camphor is a solid and nitrogen is a gas, so this is a solid in a gas (I).\n(B) Hydrogen is a gas absorbed by solid palladium, so this is a gas in a solid (II).\n(C) Mercury is a liquid and sodium is a solid, so this is a liquid in a solid (III).\n(D) Chloroform vapour is a liquid component in nitrogen gas, so this is a liquid in a gas (IV).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":35,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Water at 293 K is kept in contact with oxygen at a partial pressure of 0.20 bar. The Henry's law constant for $O_2$ in water at 293 K is 34.86 kbar. How many millimoles of $O_2$ dissolve in 1 litre of water? (Take 1 L of water = 55.5 mol, and neglect moles of $O_2$ compared with moles of water.)","image":"","options":["0.318 mmol","0.159 mmol","0.637 mmol","3.18 mmol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p = 0.20$ bar, $K_H = 34860$ bar, and $n_{water} = 55.5$ mol.\nFormula: $x = \\frac{p}{K_H}$ and $n_{O_2} \\approx x \\times n_{water}$.\nSubstituting: $x = \\frac{0.20}{34860} = 5.74 \\times 10^{-6}$.\nThen $n_{O_2} = 5.74 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 55.5 = 3.18 \\times 10^{-4}$ mol.\nSo the amount of $O_2$ dissolved = 0.318 mmol.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":36,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"50 mL of n-hexane is mixed with 50 mL of n-heptane at constant temperature. Assuming ideal behaviour, which observation is expected?","image":"","options":["Volume of mixture is less than 100 mL and heat is evolved","Volume of mixture is more than 100 mL and heat is absorbed","Volume of mixture is 100 mL and no heat is absorbed or evolved","Volume of mixture is 100 mL but heat is evolved"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"For an ideal solution, $\\Delta_{mix}H = 0$ and $\\Delta_{mix}V = 0$.\nWith $\\Delta_{mix}V = 0$, the solution volume equals the sum of the component volumes: 50 + 50 = 100 mL.\nWith $\\Delta_{mix}H = 0$, no heat is absorbed or evolved on mixing.\nOption (4) fails because it gets the volume right but wrongly gives a non-zero enthalpy of mixing.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":37,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are solid solutions?\n(i) Hydrogen in palladium\n(ii) Amalgam of mercury with sodium\n(iii) Copper dissolved in gold\n(iv) Camphor in nitrogen gas\n(v) Chloroform mixed with nitrogen gas\n(vi) Oxygen dissolved in water","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Solid solution: the solvent Pd is a solid.\n(ii) Solid solution: the solvent Na is a solid.\n(iii) Solid solution: the solvent Au is a solid.\n(iv) and (v) Gaseous solutions: the solvent N2 is a gas.\n(vi) Liquid solution: the solvent water is a liquid.\nSo the total is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":38,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following solutes will show a van't Hoff factor greater than 1 in the solvent mentioned?\nKCl in water, glucose in water, urea in water, K$_2$SO$_4$ in water, CaCl$_2$ in water, ethanoic acid in benzene, NaCl in water","image":"","options":["4","3","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"i > 1 only when the solute dissociates.\nKCl, K$_2$SO$_4$, CaCl$_2$ and NaCl are electrolytes that dissociate in water, so each has i > 1.\nGlucose and urea are non-electrolytes, so i = 1.\nEthanoic acid dimerises in benzene, so i is about 0.5 (less than 1).\nThe count is therefore 4.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":39,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (solute and condition) with List-II (van't Hoff factor, i):\nList-I\n(P) CaCl$_2$ in water, completely dissociated\n(Q) Ethanoic acid in benzene, completely dimerised\n(R) Glucose in water\n(S) Al$_2$(SO$_4$)$_3$ in water, completely dissociated\nList-II\n(i) 1\n(ii) 0.5\n(iii) 5\n(iv) 3","image":"","options":["P-(iv), Q-(ii), R-(i), S-(iii)","P-(iii), Q-(ii), R-(i), S-(iv)","P-(iv), Q-(i), R-(ii), S-(iii)","P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"P: CaCl$_2$ → Ca$^{2+}$ + 2Cl$^-$, giving 3 particles, so i = 3 (iv).\nQ: 2CH$_3$COOH → (CH$_3$COOH)$_2$, which halves the number of particles, so i = 0.5 (ii).\nR: glucose neither dissociates nor associates, so i = 1 (i).\nS: Al$_2$(SO$_4$)$_3$ → 2Al$^{3+}$ + 3SO$_4^{2-}$, giving 5 particles, so i = 5 (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":40,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A sample of brackish water is treated as a 2.925% (w/V) aqueous NaCl solution. NaCl is completely dissociated in it. What is the minimum pressure that must be exceeded on the solution side to carry out reverse osmosis at 300 K?\n(Na = 23, Cl = 35.5 g $mol^{-1}$; R = 0.083 L bar $mol^{-1}$ $K^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["12.45 bar","24.9 bar","2.49 bar","49.8 bar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 2.925 g NaCl in 100 mL, which equals 29.25 g per litre. Molar mass of NaCl = 58.5 g $mol^{-1}$. For complete dissociation, i = 2.\nMolarity: $C = \\frac{29.25}{58.5} = 0.5$ M.\nFormula: $\\Pi = iCRT$.\nSubstituting: $\\Pi = 2 \\times 0.5 \\times 0.083 \\times 300 = 24.9$ bar.\nReverse osmosis needs a pressure greater than the osmotic pressure, so the pressure must exceed 24.9 bar. Option (1) ignores dissociation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":41,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"27 g of glucose ($C_6H_{12}O_6$, molar mass 180 g $mol^{-1}$) is dissolved in 250 g of water. $K_b$ for water = 0.52 K kg $mol^{-1}$ and $K_f$ for water = 1.86 K kg $mol^{-1}$. What is the elevation in boiling point of the solution?","image":"","options":["0.312 K","0.156 K","0.078 K","1.116 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $w_2$ = 27 g, $M_2$ = 180 g $mol^{-1}$, $w_1$ = 0.250 kg, $K_b$ = 0.52 K kg $mol^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Delta T_b = K_b m$, where $m = \\frac{w_2/M_2}{w_1(kg)}$.\nSubstituting: moles of glucose = 27/180 = 0.15 mol, so m = 0.15/0.25 = 0.6 mol $kg^{-1}$.\n$\\Delta T_b$ = 0.52 × 0.6 = 0.312 K.\nOption (4) wrongly uses $K_f$ instead of $K_b$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":42,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Camphor in nitrogen gas is an example of which combination of solute and solvent?","image":"","options":["Solute: gas; Solvent: solid","Solute: solid; Solvent: gas","Solute: liquid; Solvent: gas","Solute: solid; Solvent: liquid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Camphor is a solid that sublimes, and its vapour mixes with nitrogen.\nSo camphor is the solid solute and nitrogen is the gaseous solvent. This is a gaseous solution (Table 1.1).\nOption (3) describes chloroform mixed with nitrogen, which has a liquid solute.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":43,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A 500 mL sample of lake water (density 1.02 g mL$^{-1}$) contains 4.08 mg of dissolved oxygen. What is the concentration of dissolved O$_2$ in ppm by mass?","image":"","options":["4.08 ppm","8.0 ppm","8.16 ppm","0.8 ppm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: V = 500 mL, density = 1.02 g mL$^{-1}$, mass of O$_2$ = 4.08 mg = $4.08 \\times 10^{-3}$ g.\nMass of sample = 500 × 1.02 = 510 g.\nFormula: ppm = (mass of component/total mass) × $10^6$.\nSubstituting: ppm = ($4.08 \\times 10^{-3}$/510) × $10^6$ = 8.0 ppm.\nThe value 8.16 ppm comes from wrongly taking the mass as 500 g.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":44,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following liquid mixtures show negative deviation from Raoult's law?\nPhenol + aniline, ethanol + acetone, chloroform + acetone, carbon disulphide + acetone, nitric acid + water, benzene + toluene, ethanol + water","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mixtures with negative deviation:\n- Phenol + aniline: strong O–H···N hydrogen bonding.\n- Chloroform + acetone: hydrogen bonding between the two molecules.\n- Nitric acid + water: forms a maximum boiling azeotrope, which needs a large negative deviation.\nMixtures with positive deviation: ethanol + acetone, CS₂ + acetone and ethanol + water.\nBenzene + toluene is nearly ideal.\nCount = 3.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":45,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a solution of a non-volatile solute, the relative lowering of vapour pressure of the solvent is equal to the mole fraction of the solute.\nStatement II: The relative lowering of vapour pressure depends on the chemical nature of the non-volatile solute.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: $\\frac{p_1^0 - p_1}{p_1^0} = x_2$.\nStatement II is incorrect: the lowering depends only on the number of solute particles, not on their identity. That is why it is a colligative property.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":46,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Liquids A and B form an ideal solution. At 320 K the vapour pressure of pure A is 300 mm Hg. What is the partial vapour pressure of A over a solution in which the mole fraction of A is 0.40?","image":"","options":["120 mm Hg","180 mm Hg","300 mm Hg","750 mm Hg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p_A^0$ = 300 mm Hg, $x_A$ = 0.40\nFormula: $p_A = p_A^0 x_A$ (Raoult's law)\nSubstituting: $p_A$ = 300 × 0.40\nSo $p_A$ = 120 mm Hg. The value 180 mm Hg wrongly uses $x_B$ = 0.60.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":47,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Naphthalene and anthracene hardly dissolve in water. In which of the following solvents would they dissolve most readily?","image":"","options":["Water containing dissolved sodium chloride","Benzene","Water at a higher temperature","Aqueous sugar solution"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Naphthalene and anthracene are non-polar solutes.\nA solute dissolves when its intermolecular interactions are similar to those of the solvent (like dissolves like).\nBenzene is non-polar, so these solutes dissolve readily in it.\nWater, hot water, brine and sugar solution are all polar aqueous media. They remain poor solvents for non-polar solids.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":48,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A mixture of ethanol and acetone shows positive deviation because acetone molecules break some hydrogen bonds between ethanol molecules","A mixture of chloroform and acetone shows negative deviation because chloroform forms a hydrogen bond with acetone","Solutions showing a large positive deviation from Raoult's law form maximum boiling azeotropes","For an ideal solution, $\\Delta_{mix}H = 0$ and $\\Delta_{mix}V = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Breaking hydrogen bonds weakens the attractions, so molecules escape more easily and the vapour pressure rises.\n(2) Correct. The A–B hydrogen bond lowers the escaping tendency, so the vapour pressure falls.\n(3) Incorrect. A large positive deviation gives a minimum boiling azeotrope (for example, ethanol–water). Maximum boiling azeotropes come from a large negative deviation.\n(4) Correct. These are the defining properties of an ideal solution.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":49,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In a solution showing negative deviation, A-B attractive forces are stronger than A-A and B-B forces.\n(ii) A minimum boiling azeotrope boils at a temperature lower than the boiling point of either pure component.\n(iii) The nitric acid-water azeotrope contains 68% nitric acid by volume.\n(iv) When chloroform and acetone are mixed, heat is evolved.\n(v) For an ideal solution of two volatile liquids, the vapour phase has the same composition as the liquid phase.\n(vi) The ethanol-water azeotrope contains about 95% ethanol by volume.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: stronger A-B attractions lower the escaping tendency, giving negative deviation.\n(ii) Correct: a large positive deviation raises the vapour pressure to a maximum, so the boiling point is a minimum.\n(iii) Incorrect: the azeotrope is 68% nitric acid by mass, not by volume.\n(iv) Correct: this mixture shows negative deviation, so $\\Delta_{mix}H < 0$ and heat is evolved.\n(v) Incorrect: the vapour is richer in the more volatile component.\n(vi) Correct.\nCorrect statements: (i), (ii), (iv), (vi), which makes 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":50,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"2.44 g of benzoic acid (C$_6$H$_5$COOH, molar mass 122 g mol$^{-1}$) is dissolved in 50 g of benzene. The observed depression in freezing point is 1.28 K. The acid partly forms dimers in benzene. What is its percentage degree of association? ($K_f$ for benzene = 5.12 K kg mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["75%","37.5%","62.5%","50%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: moles of acid = 2.44/122 = 0.02 mol, so m = 0.02/0.050 = 0.4 mol kg$^{-1}$.\nCalculated $\\Delta T_f$ (no association) = 5.12 × 0.4 = 2.048 K.\nFormula: i = observed/calculated = 1.28/2.048 = 0.625. For dimerisation, i = 1 − x/2.\nSubstituting: x/2 = 1 − 0.625 = 0.375, so x = 0.75.\nSo the degree of association = 75%. The value 62.5% simply mistakes i for the answer.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":51,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Mass percentage, ppm, mole fraction and molality of a solution do not change with temperature.\nStatement II: When an aqueous solution is heated, its molarity increases.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Mass %, ppm, mole fraction and molality are built only from masses or moles, and these do not depend on temperature.\nStatement II is incorrect. Molarity uses the volume of solution, and volume usually increases on heating.\nWith the same moles in a larger volume, molarity decreases, not increases.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":52,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A solvent has a vapour pressure of 40.0 mm Hg at a certain temperature. A non-volatile solute is dissolved in it so that the mole fraction of the solute is 0.05. What is the lowering of vapour pressure of the solvent?","image":"","options":["2.0 mm Hg","38.0 mm Hg","0.5 mm Hg","4.0 mm Hg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p_1^0$ = 40.0 mm Hg and $x_2$ = 0.05.\nFormula: $\\Delta p_1 = x_2 p_1^0$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta p_1 = 0.05 \\times 40.0$.\nSo the lowering is 2.0 mm Hg. Note that 38.0 mm Hg is the vapour pressure of the solution, not the lowering.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":53,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements:\n(i) A 0.9% (mass/volume) NaCl solution is isotonic with the fluid inside blood cells.\n(ii) Blood cells placed in a 1.5% (mass/volume) NaCl solution swell because water enters them.\n(iii) In reverse osmosis, a pressure greater than the osmotic pressure is applied on the solution side, and a cellulose acetate membrane lets water through but holds back ions.\n(iv) For dilute solutions, osmotic pressure is directly proportional to the molality of the solution.\n(v) Raoult's law is a special case of Henry's law in which $K_H$ equals the vapour pressure of the pure component.\nWhich statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (iii) and (iv) only","(ii), (iii) and (v) only","(i) and (v) only","(i), (iii) and (v) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. 0.9% NaCl is normal saline, which is isotonic with blood cell fluid.\n(ii) Incorrect. A 1.5% solution is hypertonic, so water flows out of the cells and they shrink.\n(iii) Correct. This is how desalination of sea water works.\n(iv) Incorrect. $\\Pi = CRT$, so osmotic pressure is proportional to molarity, not molality.\n(v) Correct. With $K_H = p_1^0$, the equation $p = K_H x$ becomes Raoult's law.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":54,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"When the equations of Raoult's law, $p_1 = x_1 p_1^0$, and Henry's law, $p = K_H x$, are compared, Raoult's law turns out to be a special case of Henry's law. In this special case, the Henry's law constant $K_H$ becomes equal to:","image":"","options":["the total vapour pressure of the solution, $p_{total}$","the vapour pressure of the pure component, $p_1^0$, at the same temperature","the mole fraction of the component in the vapour phase, $y_1$","the reciprocal of the vapour pressure of the pure component, $\\frac{1}{p_1^0}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both laws say that the partial pressure of a volatile component is directly proportional to its mole fraction in the solution.\nOnly the proportionality constant differs: it is $K_H$ in Henry's law and $p_1^0$ in Raoult's law.\nSo Raoult's law is the special case of Henry's law in which $K_H = p_1^0$.\n$p_{total}$ is wrong because the constant belongs to a single component, not to the whole mixture.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":55,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the solubility of gases in liquids are correct?\n(i) At a given partial pressure, a gas with a higher $K_H$ value is less soluble.\n(ii) $K_H$ depends only on the nature of the gas and is independent of temperature.\n(iii) Soft drink bottles are sealed under high pressure to increase the solubility of CO$_2$.\n(iv) Low partial pressure of oxygen at high altitudes can cause anoxia in climbers.\n(v) Scuba divers' tanks contain air diluted with helium to help avoid bends.\n(vi) Solubility of a gas rises with temperature because its dissolution is endothermic.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: from $x = p/K_H$, a larger $K_H$ gives a smaller $x$.\n(ii) Incorrect: $K_H$ also changes with temperature (for N$_2$ and O$_2$ it increases on heating).\n(iii) Correct: high pressure dissolves more CO$_2$.\n(iv) Correct: low $p_{O_2}$ lowers blood oxygen, which causes anoxia.\n(v) Correct: helium dilution reduces dissolved N$_2$ and helps prevent bends.\n(vi) Incorrect: dissolution of a gas is exothermic, so solubility falls as temperature rises.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":56,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"4.0 g of NaOH is dissolved in water and the volume of the solution is made up to 250 mL. What is the molarity of the solution? (Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1)","image":"","options":["0.1 M","0.016 M","1.6 M","0.4 M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: mass of NaOH = 4.0 g, molar mass = 40 g mol$^{-1}$, V = 250 mL = 0.250 L\nFormula: Molarity = moles of solute / volume of solution in litres\nSubstituting: moles = 4.0/40 = 0.1 mol; M = 0.1/0.250\nSo molarity = 0.4 M\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":57,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A well-water sample is contaminated with chloroform (CHCl$_3$) at a level of 25 ppm by mass. What is the molality of CHCl$_3$ in this sample? (C = 12, H = 1, Cl = 35.5 g mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["$2.09 \\times 10^{-1}$ m","$2.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ m","$2.09 \\times 10^{-7}$ m","$2.09 \\times 10^{-4}$ m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: 25 ppm means 25 g of CHCl$_3$ in $10^6$ g of solution.\nMolar mass of CHCl$_3$ = 12 + 1 + 3(35.5) = 119.5 g mol$^{-1}$, so moles = 25/119.5 = 0.2092 mol.\nMass of water = $10^6$ – 25 g ≈ 999.975 kg.\nFormula: molality = moles of solute/kg of solvent = 0.2092/999.975 = $2.09 \\times 10^{-4}$ mol kg$^{-1}$.\nNote that $2.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ is the mass %, not the molality.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":58,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"6.0 g of urea ($NH_2CONH_2$, molar mass 60 g mol$^{-1}$) is dissolved in 500 g of water. Taking $K_b$ for water = 0.52 K kg mol$^{-1}$, what is the elevation in boiling point?","image":"","options":["0.104 K","0.052 K","0.208 K","0.0104 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $w_2$ = 6.0 g, $M_2$ = 60 g mol$^{-1}$, $w_1$ = 500 g, $K_b$ = 0.52 K kg mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Delta T_b = K_b m$, where $m = \\frac{w_2/M_2}{w_1/1000}$.\nSubstituting: moles of urea = 6/60 = 0.1 mol, so $m$ = 0.1/0.5 = 0.2 mol kg$^{-1}$.\n$\\Delta T_b$ = 0.52 × 0.2 = 0.104 K.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":59,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For an ideal solution of two volatile liquids, the vapour in equilibrium with the liquid contains a larger mole fraction of the more volatile component than the liquid does.\nReason (R): The partial pressure of each component in the vapour equals its mole fraction in the liquid multiplied by the total vapour pressure, i.e. $p_i = x_i p_{total}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: the CHCl3–CH2Cl2 example shows that the vapour is richer in the more volatile component (CH2Cl2).\nR is false: Dalton's law gives $p_i = y_i p_{total}$, where $y_i$ is the mole fraction in the vapour, not in the liquid.\nRaoult's law instead gives $p_i = x_i p_i^0$.\nIf R were true, $y_i$ would equal $x_i$, and the vapour could never be enriched in any component.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":60,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An amalgam of mercury with sodium is classified as a solid solution even though mercury is a liquid.\nReason (R): The physical state of a solution is determined by the solvent.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT Table 1.1 lists the Na–Hg amalgam as a liquid-in-solid solution, which is a solid solution.\nR is true: NCERT states that the solvent determines the physical state of the solution.\nHere sodium, a solid, is the solvent, so the solution is solid. R therefore explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":61,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"10.0 g of a mixture of glucose (molar mass 180 g mol$^{-1}$) and urea (molar mass 60 g mol$^{-1}$) is dissolved in 180 g of water (molar mass 18 g mol$^{-1}$). The vapour pressure falls from 25.00 mm Hg to 24.75 mm Hg. Using the approximation $\\frac{\\Delta p}{p_1^0} \\approx \\frac{n_2}{n_1}$, what is the mass of glucose in the mixture?","image":"","options":["6.0 g","4.0 g","3.0 g","7.5 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: relative lowering = 0.25/25.00 = 0.01, and $n_1$ = 180/18 = 10 mol.\nFormula: total moles of solute $n_2$ ≈ 0.01 × 10 = 0.10 mol, because the lowering depends on the sum of solute moles.\nLet the mass of glucose be a g: $\\frac{a}{180} + \\frac{10-a}{60} = 0.10$.\nMultiplying by 180: a + 3(10 − a) = 18, which gives a = 6.0 g. The option 4.0 g is the mass of urea, not glucose.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":62,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Nitric acid and water form a maximum boiling azeotrope. Its approximate composition and boiling point are:","image":"","options":["95% nitric acid by volume, 351 K","68% nitric acid by mass, 393.5 K","68% nitric acid by volume, 373.15 K","32% nitric acid by mass, 393.5 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the nitric acid-water azeotrope contains about 68% nitric acid and 32% water by mass.\nIt boils at 393.5 K, which is higher than the boiling point of water.\nOption (4) swaps the two components: 32% is the water content, not the acid content.\nThe 95% by volume figure belongs to the ethanol-water azeotrope.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":63,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about solutions of non-volatile solutes is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The vapour pressure of the solution comes solely from the solvent","If several non-volatile solutes are present, the lowering of vapour pressure depends on the sum of their mole fractions","For the same mole fraction of solute, the absolute lowering of vapour pressure ($\\Delta p_1$) is the same in every solvent","A plot of the solution's vapour pressure against the mole fraction of solvent is a straight line ending at $p_1^0$ when $x_1 = 1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: a non-volatile solute contributes nothing to the vapour.\n(2) Correct: this is the NCERT statement for multiple solutes.\n(3) Incorrect: $\\Delta p_1 = x_2 p_1^0$ depends on $p_1^0$, so it differs between solvents. Only the relative lowering is the same.\n(4) Correct: $p_1 = x_1 p_1^0$ is linear and gives $p_1^0$ at $x_1 = 1$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":64,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Benzene (molar mass 78 g/mol) and toluene (molar mass 92 g/mol) form a nearly ideal solution. At a certain temperature the vapour pressures of pure benzene and pure toluene are 75 mm Hg and 25 mm Hg respectively. A solution is made by mixing 39 g of benzene with 46 g of toluene. What is the mole fraction of benzene in the vapour phase in equilibrium with this solution?","image":"","options":["0.50","0.75","0.25","0.60"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: moles of benzene = 39/78 = 0.5; moles of toluene = 46/92 = 0.5. So $x_B = x_T$ = 0.5.\nFormula: $p_i = p_i^0 x_i$ and $y_i = p_i/p_{total}$\nSubstituting: $p_B$ = 75 × 0.5 = 37.5 mm Hg; $p_T$ = 25 × 0.5 = 12.5 mm Hg; $p_{total}$ = 50 mm Hg\nSo $y_B$ = 37.5/50 = 0.75. The vapour is richer in the more volatile benzene.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":65,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When an aqueous solution of urea is heated from 20 °C to 50 °C without loss of water, its molarity decreases slightly but its molality remains unchanged.\nReason (R): The volume of a solution changes with temperature, whereas the masses of solute and solvent do not.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Molarity is moles of solute per litre of solution. On heating, the liquid expands, so the volume rises and the molarity falls slightly. Molality is moles of solute per kg of solvent, which involves only masses, so it stays the same.\nR is true. NCERT states that volume depends on temperature and mass does not.\nR directly explains why molarity is temperature dependent while molality, mole fraction and mass % are not.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":66,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The molar mass of ethanoic acid found from the depression of freezing point of its solution in benzene is nearly double its normal molar mass.\nReason (R): In benzene, a solvent of low dielectric constant, ethanoic acid molecules form dimers through hydrogen bonding, which reduces the number of solute particles.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. If ethanoic acid associates completely, $\\Delta T_f$ is about half the expected value. The molar mass calculated from it is therefore about twice the normal value, so i ≈ 0.5.\nR is true. Dimerisation through hydrogen bonding occurs in solvents of low dielectric constant such as benzene, and it halves the number of particles.\nR explains A directly.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":67,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following observations are explained by osmosis?\n(i) A raw mango shrivels when kept in concentrated brine\n(ii) Wilted flowers revive when placed in fresh water\n(iii) Puffiness (edema) in people who eat very salty food\n(iv) Bubbles of nitrogen form in the blood of a scuba diver rising quickly to the surface\n(v) Bacteria on salted meat or candied fruit shrivel and die\n(vi) Soft drink bottles are sealed under high pressure to keep more $CO_2$ dissolved","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Osmosis: water leaves the mango into the concentrated salt solution.\n(ii) Osmosis: water enters the flower cells.\n(iii) Osmosis: excess salt causes water to be retained in tissues.\n(iv) Not osmosis: bends are explained by Henry's law, because gas solubility falls as pressure falls.\n(v) Osmosis: the bacterium loses water to the salt or sugar.\n(vi) Not osmosis: this is an application of Henry's law.\nSo 4 observations are explained by osmosis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":68,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The molal elevation constant of a solvent increases as the molality of the dissolved solute increases.\nReason (R): $K_b = \\frac{R M_1 T_b^2}{1000\\,\\Delta_{vap}H}$ contains only quantities that are characteristic of the solvent.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true: $M_1$, $T_b$ and $\\Delta_{vap}H$ are the molar mass, boiling point and enthalpy of vaporisation of the solvent. R is a gas constant.\nA is false: since $K_b$ depends only on solvent properties, it is a constant for a given solvent. It does not change with the amount of solute. Only $\\Delta T_b$ changes with molality.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":69,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Based on the trend of van't Hoff factors of strong electrolytes at different molalities (0.1 m, 0.01 m, 0.001 m) given in NCERT, which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["For NaCl, the value of i at 0.001 m is closer to 2 than its value at 0.1 m","At 0.1 m, MgSO$_4$ has a value of i closer to 2 than NaCl","The value of i for K$_2$SO$_4$ decreases as the solution is diluted","At 0.01 m, the i values of KCl and NaCl differ widely"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: For NaCl, i rises from 1.87 at 0.1 m to 1.97 at 0.001 m and approaches 2 on dilution.\n(2) Incorrect: At 0.1 m, i is only 1.21 for MgSO$_4$ but 1.87 for NaCl.\n(3) Incorrect: For K$_2$SO$_4$, i increases on dilution (2.32 → 2.70 → 2.84) towards 3.\n(4) Incorrect: Both KCl and NaCl have i = 1.94 at 0.01 m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":70,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"9.0 g of glucose ($C_6H_{12}O_6$) is dissolved in 250 g of water. What is the molality of the solution? (Atomic masses: C = 12, H = 1, O = 16)","image":"","options":["0.05 mol kg$^{-1}$","0.2 mol kg$^{-1}$","0.036 mol kg$^{-1}$","2.0 mol kg$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: mass of glucose = 9.0 g, mass of water = 250 g = 0.250 kg.\nMolar mass of glucose = $6\\times12 + 12\\times1 + 6\\times16 = 180$ g mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: molality $m = \\frac{\\text{moles of solute}}{\\text{mass of solvent in kg}}$.\nSubstituting: moles of glucose = $\\frac{9.0}{180} = 0.05$ mol, so $m = \\frac{0.05}{0.250}$.\nSo m = 0.2 mol kg$^{-1}$. Option (1) is just the number of moles, not divided by the solvent mass in kg.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":71,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A mixture of chloroform and acetone shows negative deviation from Raoult's law. This is mainly because:","image":"","options":["Acetone breaks the hydrogen bonds present in pure chloroform","Chloroform forms a hydrogen bond with the carbonyl oxygen of acetone","Dipolar interactions between chloroform and acetone are weaker than those in the pure liquids","Both liquids are non-polar and have equal intermolecular forces"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The H atom of $CHCl_3$ is made acidic by the three Cl atoms. It forms a hydrogen bond with the lone pair on the C=O oxygen of acetone.\nThis new A-B interaction is stronger than the A-A and B-B interactions in the pure liquids.\nAs a result, the escaping tendency of both components falls and the vapour pressure is lower than predicted, giving negative deviation.\nOption (3) describes the cause of positive deviation, as in CS$_2$-acetone.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":72,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Drinking water with about 1 ppm of fluoride ions helps prevent tooth decay, whereas about 1.5 ppm makes teeth mottled.\nStatement II: A fluoride level of 1.5 ppm (by mass) means 1.5 g of fluoride is present per kilogram of water.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. NCERT states that 1 ppm of fluoride prevents tooth decay and 1.5 ppm causes mottling.\nStatement II is false. 1 ppm means 1 part per $10^6$ parts, so 1.5 ppm is 1.5 g per $10^6$ g. That equals 1.5 mg per kg, not 1.5 g per kg.\nA value of 1.5 g/kg would actually be 1500 ppm.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":73,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Liquids A and B form an ideal solution. At a certain temperature, $p_A^0$ = 200 mm Hg and $p_B^0$ = 600 mm Hg. The vapour in equilibrium with the solution contains A and B in equal mole fractions ($y_A = y_B$ = 0.5). What is the total vapour pressure over the solution?","image":"","options":["300 mm Hg","400 mm Hg","350 mm Hg","450 mm Hg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $y_A = y_B$ = 0.5, $p_A^0$ = 200 mm Hg, $p_B^0$ = 600 mm Hg\nFormula: combining $y_i p_{total} = x_i p_i^0$ with $x_A + x_B = 1$ gives $\\frac{1}{p_{total}} = \\frac{y_A}{p_A^0} + \\frac{y_B}{p_B^0}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{p_{total}} = \\frac{0.5}{200} + \\frac{0.5}{600} = \\frac{1}{300}$\nSo $p_{total}$ = 300 mm Hg (liquid composition: $x_A$ = 0.75). The value 400 mm Hg wrongly averages the pure vapour pressures.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":74,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A bottle containing 750 g of water is sealed at 298 K under a gas mixture at a total pressure of 5.0 bar. The mole fraction of CO$_2$ in this gas phase is 0.80. Henry's law constant for CO$_2$ in water at 298 K is 1.67 kbar. What mass of CO$_2$ is dissolved in the water? (Molar masses: H$_2$O = 18 g mol$^{-1}$, CO$_2$ = 44 g mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["4.40 g","5.49 g","2.20 g","0.0998 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p_{total} = 5.0$ bar, $y_{CO_2} = 0.80$, $K_H = 1670$ bar, $n_{water} = \\frac{750}{18} = 41.67$ mol.\nPartial pressure: $p_{CO_2} = 0.80 \\times 5.0 = 4.0$ bar.\nMole fraction in solution: $x = \\frac{4.0}{1670} = 2.40 \\times 10^{-3}$.\nMoles: $n \\approx x \\times 41.67 = 0.100$ mol.\nMass $= 0.100 \\times 44 = 4.40$ g.\nUsing the total pressure (5.0 bar) instead of the partial pressure gives the wrong value 5.49 g.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":75,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A solution is made by dissolving 2.0 g of a polymer in water to give 250 mL of solution. Its osmotic pressure at 300 K is $2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ bar. What is the molar mass of the polymer? (R = 0.083 L bar mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["99,600 g mol$^{-1}$","24,900 g mol$^{-1}$","9,960 g mol$^{-1}$","3,98,400 g mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: w$_2$ = 2.0 g, V = 0.250 L, T = 300 K, $\\Pi = 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ bar.\nFormula: $M_2 = \\frac{w_2RT}{\\Pi V}$\nSubstituting: $M_2 = \\frac{2.0 \\times 0.083 \\times 300}{2.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 0.250} = \\frac{49.8}{5.0 \\times 10^{-4}}$\nSo M$_2$ = 99,600 g mol$^{-1}$.\nForgetting to convert mL to L gives a wrong result.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":76,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Air saturates water at 293 K such that the partial pressure of O$_2$ above the water is 0.20 bar. Henry's law constant for O$_2$ in water at 293 K is 34.86 kbar. Taking 1 litre of water as 55.5 mol, how many millimoles of O$_2$ dissolve in 1 litre of water?","image":"","options":["0.318 mmol","0.159 mmol","3.18 mmol","0.0318 mmol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p = 0.20$ bar, $K_H = 34.86$ kbar $= 34860$ bar, $n_{water} = 55.5$ mol.\nFormula: $x = \\frac{p}{K_H}$, and $x \\approx \\frac{n}{55.5}$ because $n \\ll 55.5$.\nSubstituting: $x = \\frac{0.20}{34860} = 5.74 \\times 10^{-6}$.\nThen $n = 5.74 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 55.5 = 3.18 \\times 10^{-4}$ mol.\nSo the amount of O$_2$ dissolved $= 0.318$ mmol.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":77,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Two volatile liquids A and B form an ideal solution. At a given temperature, $p_A^0$ = 150 mm Hg and $p_B^0$ = 50 mm Hg. The vapour in equilibrium with a certain solution contains A at a mole fraction of 0.60. What is the mole fraction of A in the liquid solution?","image":"","options":["0.333","0.600","0.500","0.200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $y_A$ = 0.60, $p_A^0$ = 150 mm Hg, $p_B^0$ = 50 mm Hg\nFormula: $y_A = \\frac{x_A p_A^0}{x_A p_A^0 + (1 - x_A)p_B^0}$\nSubstituting: $0.6 = \\frac{150x_A}{100x_A + 50}$, so $60x_A + 30 = 150x_A$\nSo $x_A$ = 30/90 = 0.333. This is less than 0.60, as expected: the liquid holds less of the volatile A than the vapour does.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":78,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Two beakers at 30 °C contain saturated aqueous solutions with some excess undissolved solid at the bottom. Beaker P has solid X ($\\Delta_{sol}H > 0$) and beaker Q has solid Y ($\\Delta_{sol}H < 0$). Both beakers are heated to 50 °C at constant pressure, and equilibrium is re-established. Which observation is expected?","image":"","options":["More X dissolves in P; some Y crystallises out in Q","Some X crystallises out in P; more Y dissolves in Q","More solid dissolves in both beakers","No change occurs in either beaker because both are already saturated"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each beaker holds a dynamic equilibrium, so Le Chatelier's principle applies.\nX (endothermic dissolution): heating favours the forward process. Its solubility rises, so more of the excess X dissolves.\nY (exothermic dissolution): heating favours the reverse process. Its solubility falls, so some Y crystallises out.\nOption (4) fails because saturation holds only at a given temperature; a temperature change shifts the equilibrium.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":79,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Liquids A and B form an ideal solution. At a certain temperature, the vapour pressure of pure A is 400 mm Hg and that of pure B is 600 mm Hg. What is the mole fraction of B in the vapour phase that is in equilibrium with a liquid having $x_B = 0.4$?","image":"","options":["0.40","0.50","0.60","0.30"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $p_A^0$ = 400 mm Hg, $p_B^0$ = 600 mm Hg, $x_B$ = 0.4, $x_A$ = 0.6.\nFormula: $p_i = x_i p_i^0$ and $y_B = p_B/p_{total}$.\nSubstituting: $p_A$ = 0.6 × 400 = 240 mm Hg; $p_B$ = 0.4 × 600 = 240 mm Hg; $p_{total}$ = 480 mm Hg.\nSo $y_B$ = 240/480 = 0.50. The vapour is richer in the more volatile component B than the liquid is (0.50 > 0.40).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":80,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (example) with List-II (solute–solvent type):\nList-I\n(A) Chloroform mixed with nitrogen gas\n(B) Amalgam of mercury with sodium\n(C) Oxygen dissolved in water\n(D) Copper dissolved in gold\nList-II\n(I) Gas in liquid\n(II) Solid in solid\n(III) Liquid in gas\n(IV) Liquid in solid","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Chloroform is a liquid solute in nitrogen gas, so it is liquid in gas (III).\n(B) Mercury is a liquid solute in solid sodium, so it is liquid in solid (IV).\n(C) Oxygen gas in water is gas in liquid (I).\n(D) Copper in gold is solid in solid (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":81,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Equal molal amounts of acetic acid and trifluoroacetic acid are dissolved separately in water. Trifluoroacetic acid produces the larger depression in freezing point.\nReason (R): The strongly electron-withdrawing fluorine atoms make trifluoroacetic acid a stronger acid. It therefore dissociates to a greater extent, giving a larger van't Hoff factor.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: $\\Delta T_f = iK_f m$. For the same m, the solute with the larger i gives the larger depression.\nR is true: three F atoms pull electron density away and stabilise the anion. CF$_3$COOH is therefore far more acidic than CH$_3$COOH.\nGreater dissociation produces more particles, so i is larger. This directly explains A.\nThe observed order is acetic < trichloroacetic < trifluoroacetic acid.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":82,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Molality of a solution does not change when the solution is heated from 298 K to 318 K.\nReason (R): Molality is defined using the mass of the solvent, and mass does not depend on temperature.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Molality is moles of solute per kilogram of solvent. Both quantities are based on mass, so A is true.\nMasses do not change with temperature, while volumes do. This is why molarity changes on heating but molality does not, so R is true.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":83,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"The van't Hoff factor of CaCl$_2$ in a certain aqueous solution is 2.47. What is the percentage degree of dissociation of CaCl$_2$ in this solution?","image":"","options":["73.5%","49.0%","147%","82.3%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: CaCl$_2$ → Ca$^{2+}$ + 2Cl$^-$, so n = 3 particles per formula unit; i = 2.47.\nFormula: From i = 1 − α + nα = 1 + (n − 1)α, we get α = $\\frac{i-1}{n-1}$.\nSubstituting: α = (2.47 − 1)/(3 − 1) = 1.47/2 = 0.735.\nSo the degree of dissociation = 73.5%. Dividing by 3 instead of 2 gives the wrong value of 49%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":84,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"25 mL of 0.80 M $H_2SO_4$ is diluted with water until the total volume of the solution becomes 400 mL. What is the molarity of the diluted acid?","image":"","options":["0.05 M","0.10 M","0.032 M","12.8 M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $M_1 = 0.80$ M, $V_1 = 25$ mL, $V_2 = 400$ mL (final total volume).\nFormula: on dilution the moles of solute do not change, so $M_1V_1 = M_2V_2$.\nSubstituting: $M_2 = \\frac{0.80 \\times 25}{400}$.\nSo $M_2$ = 0.05 M. Option (4), 12.8 M, comes from inverting the ratio, which would mean the solution became more concentrated on adding water.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":85,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The value of $K_H$ at a given temperature depends on the nature of the gas.\n(ii) At a given partial pressure, a higher value of $K_H$ means a higher solubility of the gas.\n(iii) $K_H$ of O$_2$ in water increases when the temperature rises from 293 K to 303 K.\n(iv) Raoult's law is a special case of Henry's law in which $K_H$ equals the vapour pressure of the pure component.\n(v) Increasing pressure significantly increases the solubility of solids in liquids.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: different gases have different $K_H$ values at the same temperature.\n(ii) Incorrect: since $x = \\frac{p}{K_H}$, a higher $K_H$ means lower solubility.\n(iii) Correct: $K_H$ of O$_2$ rises from 34.86 kbar to 46.82 kbar.\n(iv) Correct: comparing $p = K_H x$ with $p_1 = p_1^0 x_1$ gives $K_H = p_1^0$.\n(v) Incorrect: solids and liquids are nearly incompressible, so pressure has no significant effect.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":86,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When an aqueous NaCl solution is heated, its molarity decreases slightly but its molality remains unchanged.\nStatement II: In a binary solution, the mole fraction of the solvent is equal to 1 minus the mole fraction of the solute.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. On heating, the volume of the solution increases, so moles per litre (molarity) fall. Molality uses the mass of the solvent, which does not change.\nStatement II is correct. The sum of all mole fractions in a solution is unity, so for a binary solution x$_1$ + x$_2$ = 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":87,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): To prepare 100 mL of a 10% (V/V) ethanol solution, 10 mL of ethanol is diluted with water up to a total volume of 100 mL, instead of simply being added to exactly 90 mL of water.\nReason (R): Ethanol and water form a non-ideal solution, for which the volume of mixing ($\\Delta_{mix}V$) is not zero.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Volume % is defined with respect to the total volume of the solution. So the ethanol is made up to 100 mL of solution.\nR is true. Ethanol–water deviates from Raoult's law and forms an azeotrope, so it is non-ideal. Only ideal solutions have $\\Delta_{mix}V = 0$.\nBecause the volumes are not additive, 10 mL + 90 mL would not give exactly 100 mL. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":88,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of liquids forms a nearly ideal solution?","image":"","options":["Ethanol and acetone","Benzene and toluene","Phenol and aniline","Chloroform and acetone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An ideal solution forms when the A-B attractions are nearly equal to the A-A and B-B attractions.\nBenzene and toluene are similar non-polar molecules, so NCERT lists this pair as nearly ideal.\nEthanol-acetone shows positive deviation because hydrogen bonds in ethanol are broken.\nPhenol-aniline and chloroform-acetone show negative deviation because new, stronger A-B hydrogen bonds form.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":89,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Of the nine types of binary solutions listed in NCERT Table 1.1, in how many is the solute in a different physical state from the solvent?","image":"","options":["3","4","6","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"There are 3 possible solute states and 3 possible solvent states, giving 3 × 3 = 9 types.\nIn three types the solute and solvent are in the same state: gas–gas (O2 + N2), liquid–liquid (ethanol in water) and solid–solid (Cu in Au).\nThe remaining 9 − 3 = 6 types have different states, for example H2 in Pd and glucose in water.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":90,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) For a solution showing positive deviation from Raoult's law, mixing absorbs heat and the volume of the solution is greater than the sum of the volumes of the components.\n(b) For a chloroform–acetone solution, the total vapour pressure at any intermediate composition is lower than that predicted by Raoult's law.\n(c) A maximum boiling azeotrope can be separated into its pure components by fractional distillation.\n(d) In a carbon disulphide–acetone solution, the solute–solvent dipolar interactions are stronger than the solute–solute and solvent–solvent interactions.\n(e) For an ideal solution, the volume of the solution equals the sum of the volumes of the pure components mixed.\n(f) Solutions showing a large negative deviation from Raoult's law form minimum boiling azeotropes.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: A–B attractions are weaker, so $\\Delta_{mix}H > 0$ and $\\Delta_{mix}V > 0$.\n(b) Correct: A–B hydrogen bonding lowers the escaping tendency, giving negative deviation.\n(c) Incorrect: liquid and vapour of an azeotrope have the same composition, so fractional distillation cannot separate it.\n(d) Incorrect: these interactions are weaker, which is why CS$_2$–acetone shows positive deviation.\n(e) Correct: an ideal solution has $\\Delta_{mix}V = 0$.\n(f) Incorrect: a large negative deviation gives a maximum boiling azeotrope.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":91,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"200 mL of 0.5 M $H_2SO_4$ is mixed with 300 mL of 0.2 M NaOH. Assuming the volumes are additive and $H_2SO_4$ ionises completely, what is the molar concentration of $H^+$ ions left in the final solution?","image":"","options":["0.08 M","0.14 M","0.40 M","0.28 M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: H$_2$SO$_4$ = 0.200 L × 0.5 M = 0.1 mol, which supplies 0.2 mol H$^+$ because it is diprotic.\nNaOH = 0.300 L × 0.2 M = 0.06 mol OH$^-$\nH$^+$ left = 0.2 − 0.06 = 0.14 mol in a total volume of 0.500 L\nSo [H$^+$] = 0.14/0.5 = 0.28 M. The value 0.08 M comes from wrongly treating H$_2$SO$_4$ as monoprotic.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":92,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"In a binary solution, which statement about the solvent is correct according to NCERT?","image":"","options":["It is always a liquid","It is generally the component present in the largest quantity, and it determines the physical state of the solution","It is always the component with the higher molar mass","It is the component present in the smaller quantity, and it determines the colour of the solution"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says the solvent is generally the component present in the largest quantity.\nThe solvent determines the physical state in which the solution exists.\nOption (1) fails because solvents can be gases (nitrogen in air-type mixtures) or solids (gold, palladium).\nOptions (3) and (4) are not part of the definition.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":93,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The osmotic pressure method is particularly suited for finding the molar masses of proteins and other biomolecules.\nReason (R): Osmotic pressure measurements are carried out at the boiling point of the solvent, where the solute dissolves best.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that biomolecules are generally unstable at higher temperatures, and the osmotic pressure method suits them. Its magnitude is also large even for very dilute solutions.\nR is false. Osmotic pressure is measured at around room temperature, not at the boiling point. Room-temperature measurement is precisely what makes the method safe for proteins.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":94,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following best describes a saturated solution of a solid in a liquid at a given temperature and pressure?","image":"","options":["A solution in which the solute has stopped dissolving and no particles leave the solid","A solution that can still dissolve more solute at the same temperature","A solution in dynamic equilibrium with undissolved solute, containing the maximum amount of dissolved solute","A solution whose concentration keeps rising slowly with time"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A saturated solution is in dynamic equilibrium with undissolved solute.\nDissolution and crystallisation both continue at equal rates, so the concentration stays constant. That constant concentration is the solubility.\nOption (1) is wrong because the equilibrium is dynamic, not static.\nOption (2) describes an unsaturated solution.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":95,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Henry's law constants ($K_H$) in water at 293 K are: He = 144.97 kbar, $H_2$ = 69.16 kbar, $N_2$ = 76.48 kbar and $O_2$ = 34.86 kbar. At the same partial pressure, what is the correct order of increasing solubility in water?","image":"","options":["$O_2 < H_2 < N_2 < He$","$He < N_2 < H_2 < O_2$","$He < H_2 < N_2 < O_2$","$H_2 < He < O_2 < N_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"From $p = K_H x$, we get $x = p/K_H$. At a fixed p, a higher $K_H$ means a lower solubility.\nThe $K_H$ order is He (144.97) > $N_2$ (76.48) > $H_2$ (69.16) > $O_2$ (34.86).\nSolubility therefore increases in the order He < $N_2$ < $H_2$ < $O_2$.\nOption (1) reverses this by wrongly assuming that a larger $K_H$ means greater solubility.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":96,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) In osmosis, solvent molecules flow from the solution of lower concentration to that of higher concentration.\n(b) For dilute solutions, osmotic pressure is proportional to the molality of the solution.\n(c) Two isotonic solutions separated by a semipermeable membrane show no osmosis.\n(d) To prevent osmosis, the extra pressure must be applied on the pure solvent side.\n(e) Compared with other colligative properties, osmotic pressure has a large magnitude even for very dilute solutions.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: solvent always moves from lower to higher solute concentration.\n(b) Incorrect: $\\Pi = CRT$ uses molarity, not molality.\n(c) Correct: isotonic solutions have equal osmotic pressure, so there is no net flow.\n(d) Incorrect: the excess pressure is applied on the solution side.\n(e) Correct: this is an advantage of the osmotic pressure method.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":97,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Commercial bleaching solution contains 3.62% (w/w) sodium hypochlorite in water. What mass of NaOCl is present in 500 g of this bleach?","image":"","options":["18.1 g","3.62 g","36.2 g","13.8 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: mass % of NaOCl = 3.62%, mass of solution = 500 g.\nFormula: mass of component = (mass % × total mass of solution)/100.\nSubstituting: mass = 3.62 × 500/100 = 18.1 g.\nSo the bleach contains 18.1 g of NaOCl. The value 3.62 g would be correct only for 100 g of solution.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":98,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A 0.5 molal solution of a non-volatile, non-electrolyte solute is prepared in benzene (molar mass 78 g mol$^{-1}$). Pure benzene has a vapour pressure of 100.0 mm Hg at this temperature. Using the exact Raoult's law expression (do not neglect the solute moles), what is the vapour pressure of the solution?","image":"","options":["96.2 mm Hg","99.5 mm Hg","92.5 mm Hg","3.75 mm Hg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 0.5 mol of solute per 1000 g of benzene, so $n_1$ = 1000/78 = 12.82 mol and $n_2$ = 0.5 mol.\nFormula: $p_1 = x_1 p_1^0 = \\frac{n_1}{n_1+n_2} p_1^0$.\nSubstituting: $x_1$ = 12.82/13.32 = 0.9625, so $p_1$ = 0.9625 × 100.0.\nSo $p_1$ ≈ 96.2 mm Hg. The value 3.75 mm Hg is the lowering, not the vapour pressure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":99,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"According to the vapour pressure–temperature diagram for freezing point depression (Fig. 1.8), a solution containing a non-volatile solute freezes at the temperature at which:","image":"","options":["its vapour pressure equals the atmospheric pressure","its vapour pressure equals the vapour pressure of the pure solid solvent","its vapour pressure equals the vapour pressure of the pure liquid solvent","the vapour pressure of the pure solvent becomes zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At the freezing point, the solid and liquid phases are in dynamic equilibrium, so their vapour pressures are equal.\nA solution freezes when its vapour pressure equals that of the pure solid solvent.\nBecause the solute lowers the vapour pressure, this crossing happens at a lower temperature, so $\\Delta T_f$ appears.\nOption (1) describes the condition for boiling, not freezing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":100,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"25 mL of 2.0 M $H_2SO_4$ is diluted with water to a final volume of 500 mL. What is the molarity of the diluted acid?","image":"","options":["0.2 M","0.1 M","0.05 M","1.0 M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: M$_1$ = 2.0 M, V$_1$ = 25 mL, V$_2$ = 500 mL\nFormula: on dilution the moles of solute stay the same, so M$_1$V$_1$ = M$_2$V$_2$\nSubstituting: M$_2$ = (2.0 × 25)/500\nSo M$_2$ = 0.1 M\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":101,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"9.0 g of glucose ($C_6H_{12}O_6$, molar mass 180 g mol$^{-1}$) is dissolved in 250 g of water. What is the molality of the solution?","image":"","options":["0.2 mol kg$^{-1}$","0.05 mol kg$^{-1}$","0.036 mol kg$^{-1}$","2.0 mol kg$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: w$_2$ = 9.0 g, M$_2$ = 180 g mol$^{-1}$, mass of water = 250 g = 0.250 kg\nFormula: m = moles of solute / mass of solvent in kg\nSubstituting: moles = 9.0/180 = 0.05 mol; m = 0.05/0.250\nSo molality = 0.2 mol kg$^{-1}$\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":102,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The Henry's law constant for $O_2$ in water is larger at 303 K (46.82 kbar) than at 293 K (34.86 kbar).\nReason (R): Dissolution of a gas in a liquid is an exothermic process, similar to condensation, so the solubility of the gas decreases as temperature rises.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The NCERT table gives $K_H(O_2)$ = 34.86 kbar at 293 K and 46.82 kbar at 303 K.\nR is true. Gas molecules entering the liquid release heat, much like condensation. By Le Chatelier's principle, raising the temperature therefore lowers the solubility.\nLower solubility at the same pressure means a smaller $x$. Since $K_H = \\frac{p}{x}$, $K_H$ must increase. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":103,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following solids dissolve readily in water?\nSodium chloride, sugar (sucrose), naphthalene, anthracene, glucose, urea","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Water is polar, so polar or ionic solutes dissolve in it.\nSodium chloride is ionic: soluble.\nSugar, glucose and urea are polar and can hydrogen bond with water: soluble.\nNaphthalene and anthracene are non-polar hydrocarbons: they do not dissolve readily.\nSo the count is 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":104,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"At a certain temperature, the vapour pressures of pure benzene and pure toluene are 75 mm Hg and 25 mm Hg respectively. An equimolar liquid mixture of the two forms an ideal solution. What is the mole fraction of benzene in the vapour in equilibrium with this liquid?","image":"","options":["0.25","0.50","0.60","0.75"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $x_{benzene} = x_{toluene}$ = 0.5, $p^0_{benzene}$ = 75 mm Hg, $p^0_{toluene}$ = 25 mm Hg.\nFormula: $p_i = x_i p_i^0$ and $y_i = \\frac{p_i}{p_{total}}$.\nSubstituting: $p_{benzene}$ = 37.5 mm Hg and $p_{toluene}$ = 12.5 mm Hg, so $p_{total}$ = 50 mm Hg.\nSo $y_{benzene}$ = 37.5/50 = 0.75. The vapour is richer in benzene, the more volatile component.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":105,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (solute and solvent) with List-II (approximate value of van't Hoff factor i).\nList-I:\n(A) KCl in very dilute aqueous solution\n(B) Ethanoic acid in benzene\n(C) Urea in water\n(D) K$_2$SO$_4$ in very dilute aqueous solution\nList-II:\n(i) about 0.5\n(ii) about 3\n(iii) about 2\n(iv) 1","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) KCl → K$^+$ + Cl$^-$ gives 2 ions, so i approaches 2 on dilution: (iii).\n(B) Ethanoic acid dimerises in benzene, halving the number of particles: i ≈ 0.5, (i).\n(C) Urea is a non-electrolyte that neither dissociates nor associates: i = 1, (iv).\n(D) K$_2$SO$_4$ → 2K$^+$ + SO$_4^{2-}$ gives 3 ions: i approaches 3, (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":106,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $K_b$ of a solvent depends on the nature of the solvent and not on the nature of the solute.\n(ii) For every solvent listed in NCERT Table 1.3, $K_f$ is greater than $K_b$.\n(iii) In a solution, the vapour pressure curve of the liquid meets the vapour pressure curve of the pure solid solvent at a temperature lower than the normal freezing point.\n(iv) For dilute solutions, $\\Delta T_f$ is directly proportional to the molarity of the solution.\n(v) A solution of 1 mol of sucrose in 1000 g of water boils at about 373.52 K at one atmosphere.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $K_b = \\frac{R M_1 T_b^2}{1000\\,\\Delta_{vap}H}$ contains only properties of the solvent.\n(ii) Incorrect: for diethyl ether, $K_f$ = 1.79 is less than $K_b$ = 2.02.\n(iii) Correct: the lowered vapour pressure meets the solid's curve at a lower temperature, as shown in Fig. 1.8.\n(iv) Incorrect: $\\Delta T_f = K_f m$ uses molality, not molarity.\n(v) Correct: this is the value stated in NCERT.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":107,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"n-Hexane and n-heptane form a nearly ideal solution. At 298 K, the vapour pressures of pure n-hexane and pure n-heptane are 151 mm Hg and 45.8 mm Hg respectively. What is the total vapour pressure of a solution made by mixing 2 mol of n-hexane with 3 mol of n-heptane?","image":"","options":["108.9 mm Hg","98.4 mm Hg","87.9 mm Hg","196.8 mm Hg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $p^0_{hex}$ = 151 mm Hg, $p^0_{hep}$ = 45.8 mm Hg, n(hexane) = 2 mol, n(heptane) = 3 mol.\nMole fractions: $x_{hex} = \\frac{2}{5} = 0.4$ and $x_{hep} = \\frac{3}{5} = 0.6$.\nFormula (Raoult's law with Dalton's law): $p_{total} = x_{hex}p^0_{hex} + x_{hep}p^0_{hep}$.\nSubstituting: $p_{total}$ = 0.4 × 151 + 0.6 × 45.8 = 60.4 + 27.48.\nSo $p_{total}$ = 87.9 mm Hg. Option (1) results from swapping the two mole fractions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":108,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"In the desalination of sea water by reverse osmosis, which material is commonly used as the workable porous membrane placed over a suitable support?","image":"","options":["Parchment paper","A film of cellulose acetate","Pig's bladder","Copper ferrocyanide gel"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: a workable membrane for reverse osmosis is a film of cellulose acetate placed over a suitable support.\nIt lets water pass but holds back the impurities and ions in sea water. This is why pure water is squeezed out when a pressure larger than the osmotic pressure is applied.\nParchment and pig's bladder are natural semipermeable membranes, but NCERT names them only as examples in osmosis, not as desalination membranes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":109,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are colligative properties?\nRelative lowering of vapour pressure, depression of freezing point, viscosity of the solution, elevation of boiling point, osmotic pressure, vapour pressure of the pure solvent","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Colligative properties depend only on the number of solute particles relative to the total number of particles, not on their nature.\nThe four colligative properties are relative lowering of vapour pressure, depression of freezing point, elevation of boiling point and osmotic pressure.\nViscosity depends on the nature of the substances, so it is not colligative.\nThe vapour pressure of the pure solvent involves no solute at all, so it is not colligative either.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":110,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements:\n(i) A solution showing a large positive deviation from Raoult's law forms a minimum boiling azeotrope at a specific composition.\n(ii) For a solution showing positive deviation, $\\Delta_{mix}H$ is positive.\n(iii) Fractional distillation of a fermented ethanol–water mixture gives at best about 95% ethanol by volume.\n(iv) A maximum boiling azeotrope boils at a temperature lower than the boiling points of both of its components.\n(v) At the azeotropic composition, the vapour is richer in the more volatile component than the liquid is.\nThe correct statements are:","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(i) and (iv) only","(ii), (iii) and (v) only","(i), (iii) and (v) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a large positive deviation means a vapour pressure maximum, which gives a minimum boiling azeotrope.\n(ii) Correct: A–B attractions are weaker than A–A and B–B, so mixing absorbs heat and $\\Delta_{mix}H > 0$.\n(iii) Correct: ethanol–water reaches an azeotrope at about 95% v/v ethanol, and separation stops there.\n(iv) Incorrect: a maximum boiling azeotrope boils above both components, e.g. HNO$_3$–water at 393.5 K.\n(v) Incorrect: at the azeotrope, liquid and vapour have the same composition.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":111,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about concentration units are correct?\n(i) A 10% (w/w) aqueous glucose solution contains 10 g of glucose dissolved in 90 g of water.\n(ii) A 35% (v/v) ethylene glycol antifreeze is made by dissolving 35 mL of ethylene glycol in 100 mL of water.\n(iii) Concentrations expressed as mass percentage and as ppm (mass to mass) do not change with temperature.\n(iv) Mass by volume percentage (w/V) of a solution is independent of temperature.\n(v) Mixing 100 g of a 10% (w/w) glucose solution with 100 g of a 30% (w/w) glucose solution gives a 20% (w/w) glucose solution.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: 10 g of glucose in 90 g of water makes 100 g of solution.\n(ii) Incorrect: 35% (v/v) means 35 mL of glycol in 100 mL of total solution, not in 100 mL of water.\n(iii) Correct: both units are based only on masses, and mass does not depend on temperature.\n(iv) Incorrect: w/V depends on the volume of solution, and volume changes with temperature.\n(v) Correct: glucose = 10 g + 30 g = 40 g in 200 g of solution, which is 20%.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":112,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"The van't Hoff factors of some strong electrolytes are given below.\nNaCl: 1.87 (0.1 m), 1.94 (0.01 m), 1.97 (0.001 m)\nKCl: 1.85, 1.94, 1.98\nMgSO$_4$: 1.21, 1.53, 1.82\nK$_2$SO$_4$: 2.32, 2.70, 2.84\nWhich of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["For NaCl, i increases as the solution is made more concentrated from 0.001 m to 0.1 m","For K$_2$SO$_4$, i approaches 2 as the solution becomes very dilute","At 0.1 m, MgSO$_4$ shows the largest departure from its value of i for complete dissociation","At 0.01 m, the value of i for KCl is smaller than that for NaCl"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: i for NaCl falls from 1.97 to 1.87 on concentrating.\n(2) Incorrect: K$_2$SO$_4$ gives 3 ions, so i approaches 3.\n(3) Correct: the departures from the ideal value are MgSO$_4$ 2 − 1.21 = 0.79, K$_2$SO$_4$ 3 − 2.32 = 0.68, NaCl 0.13 and KCl 0.15. The largest is for MgSO$_4$, where attraction between doubly charged ions is strong.\n(4) Incorrect: both values are 1.94.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":113,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (observation) with List-II (explanation based on osmosis).\nList-I\n(A) Raw mango kept in brine shrivels\n(B) Limp carrot placed in water becomes firm\n(C) Puffiness in people eating a lot of salty food\n(D) Bacteria on salted meat die\nList-II\n(i) Water retention in tissue cells and intercellular spaces (edema)\n(ii) Water moves into the cells from the surrounding water\n(iii) The microorganism loses water, shrivels and dies\n(iv) Fruit cells lose water to the concentrated salt solution","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(iv): Brine is concentrated, so water leaves the mango cells and the mango shrivels.\n(B)-(ii): Water enters the carrot cells by osmosis and the carrot becomes firm.\n(C)-(i): High salt intake causes water retention in tissues, giving puffiness called edema.\n(D)-(iii): A bacterium on salted meat loses water, shrivels and dies.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":114,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"The van't Hoff factor of a 0.1 m aqueous K$_2$SO$_4$ solution is 2.32. What is the depression in freezing point of this solution? ($K_f$ for water = 1.86 K kg mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["0.432 K","0.186 K","0.558 K","0.372 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.1 mol kg$^{-1}$, i = 2.32, $K_f$ = 1.86 K kg mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Delta T_f = i K_f m$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta T_f$ = 2.32 × 1.86 × 0.1.\nSo $\\Delta T_f$ ≈ 0.432 K.\n0.558 K wrongly assumes complete dissociation (i = 3). 0.186 K ignores dissociation altogether (i = 1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":115,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following ways of expressing concentration are independent of temperature?\nMass percentage, mole fraction, molality, molarity, mass by volume percentage","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mass percentage depends only on masses, so it is temperature independent.\nMole fraction depends only on moles, so it is temperature independent.\nMolality uses moles of solute and the mass of solvent, so it is temperature independent.\nMolarity contains the solution volume, which changes with temperature.\nMass by volume percentage also contains a volume, so it changes with temperature.\nThat gives 3 temperature-independent units.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":116,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A non-volatile, non-electrolyte solute dissolved in water gives a relative lowering of vapour pressure of 0.009. A solution of the same molality is then made in benzene (molar mass 78 g mol$^{-1}$; water 18 g mol$^{-1}$). What is the approximate relative lowering of vapour pressure of the benzene solution?","image":"","options":["0.038","0.009","0.0021","0.38"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: in water, $\\frac{n_2}{n_1}$ = 0.009/0.991 ≈ 0.00908. For molality m, $\\frac{n_2}{n_1} = \\frac{m M_1}{1000}$, so m ≈ 0.505 mol kg$^{-1}$.\nIn benzene at the same m: $\\frac{n_2}{n_1}$ = 0.505 × 78/1000 ≈ 0.0394.\nThen $x_2 = \\frac{0.0394}{1.0394}$ ≈ 0.038.\nSo the relative lowering is ≈ 0.038. It is not 0.009, because equal molality does not mean equal mole fraction when the solvents have different molar masses.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":117,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): At 1.013 bar, an aqueous solution of sucrose boils at a temperature higher than 373.15 K.\nReason (R): At 373.15 K, the vapour pressure of the aqueous sucrose solution is less than 1.013 bar.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a non-volatile solute raises the boiling point, as shown in Fig. 1.7.\nR is true: sucrose lowers the vapour pressure of water, so at 373.15 K the solution's vapour pressure is below 1.013 bar.\nA liquid boils only when its vapour pressure equals the external pressure. The solution must therefore be heated above 373.15 K to reach 1.013 bar.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":118,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A sample of sulphuric acid is 49% $H_2SO_4$ by mass and has a density of 1.4 g mL$^{-1}$. What is its molarity? (molar mass of $H_2SO_4$ = 98 g mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["5.0 M","7.0 M","9.8 M","3.5 M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 49% w/w, d = 1.4 g mL$^{-1}$, M = 98 g mol$^{-1}$\nMass of 1 L of solution = 1000 × 1.4 = 1400 g; mass of H$_2$SO$_4$ in it = 0.49 × 1400 = 686 g\nMoles = 686/98 = 7.0 mol in 1 L\nSo molarity = 7.0 M. The value 9.8 is the molality, and 5.0 results from ignoring the density.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":119,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (mixture) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\n(A) n-Hexane + n-heptane\n(B) Ethanol + acetone\n(C) Chloroform + acetone\n(D) Ethanol + water (about 95% ethanol by volume)\nList-II:\n(i) Hydrogen bonds between molecules of one component are partly broken; vapour pressure higher than Raoult's law value\n(ii) A–B attractions nearly equal to A–A and B–B attractions; $\\Delta_{mix}H \\approx 0$\n(iii) Constant-boiling mixture whose liquid and vapour have the same composition and which boils below both pure components\n(iv) New hydrogen bond forms between the two different molecules; vapour pressure lower than Raoult's law value\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) n-Hexane and n-heptane have similar interactions, so the solution is nearly ideal: (ii).\n(B) Acetone gets between ethanol molecules and breaks some of their hydrogen bonds. This gives positive deviation: (i).\n(C) Chloroform's C–H forms a hydrogen bond with the C=O of acetone. This gives negative deviation: (iv).\n(D) The 95% ethanol–water mixture is a minimum boiling azeotrope, which results from a large positive deviation: (iii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":120,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the solubility of solids in liquids is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Sodium chloride and sugar, though readily soluble in water, do not dissolve readily in benzene","If the dissolution of a solid in a nearly saturated solution is exothermic, cooling the solution increases its solubility","Raising the pressure from 1 bar to 10 bar markedly increases the solubility of sugar in water, just as it increases the solubility of $CO_2$","The concentration of solute in a solution that is in dynamic equilibrium with undissolved solute equals the solubility at that temperature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: polar and ionic solutes do not dissolve in non-polar benzene (like dissolves like).\n(2) Correct: when $\\Delta_{sol}H < 0$, lowering the temperature favours dissolution by Le Chatelier's principle.\n(3) Incorrect: pressure greatly affects gas solubility (Henry's law). It has no significant effect on solids, because solids and liquids are practically incompressible.\n(4) Correct: a solution in equilibrium with excess solute is saturated, and its concentration is the solubility.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":121,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Osmotic pressure measurement is the preferred colligative method for determining the molar masses of proteins.\nReason (R): Biomolecules are generally not stable at higher temperatures, and osmotic pressure is measured around room temperature.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the osmotic pressure method is widely used for proteins, polymers and other macromolecules.\nR is true. Biomolecules are generally unstable at high temperatures, and osmotic pressure is measured near room temperature.\nBoiling point elevation, by contrast, needs heating to the solvent's boiling point.\nR therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":122,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (mixture) with List-II (behaviour):\nList-I\n(A) n-Hexane + n-heptane\n(B) Ethanol + acetone\n(C) Phenol + aniline\n(D) Carbon disulphide + acetone\nList-II\n(p) Negative deviation due to O–H···N hydrogen bonding\n(q) Positive deviation due to weaker solute-solvent dipolar interactions\n(r) Nearly ideal behaviour\n(s) Positive deviation due to breaking of hydrogen bonds of the host liquid\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-r, B-s, C-p, D-q","A-r, B-q, C-p, D-s","A-p, B-s, C-r, D-q","A-r, B-s, C-q, D-p"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Hexane and heptane are similar non-polar alkanes, so the mixture is nearly ideal: r.\n(B) Acetone molecules get between ethanol molecules and break some of their hydrogen bonds, giving positive deviation: s.\n(C) The phenolic H forms a hydrogen bond with the lone pair on the N of aniline. This is stronger than the like-like interactions, giving negative deviation: p.\n(D) In CS$_2$-acetone, solute-solvent dipolar interactions are weaker than the pure-liquid interactions, giving positive deviation: q.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":123,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"What is the osmotic pressure of a 0.10 M aqueous glucose solution at 300 K? (R = 0.083 L bar mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["2.49 bar","0.249 bar","24.9 bar","4.98 bar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: C = 0.10 mol L$^{-1}$, T = 300 K, R = 0.083 L bar mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. Glucose is a non-electrolyte.\nFormula: $\\Pi = CRT$\nSubstituting: $\\Pi = 0.10 \\times 0.083 \\times 300$\nSo $\\Pi$ = 2.49 bar.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":124,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assume 1 mol of KCl dissociates completely in water and interionic attractions are ignored. A student does not know about this dissociation and calculates the molar mass of KCl from the observed elevation of boiling point. What molar mass will the student get? (Normal molar mass of KCl = 74.5 g mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["37.25 g mol$^{-1}$","74.5 g mol$^{-1}$","149 g mol$^{-1}$","24.83 g mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: KCl → K$^+$ + Cl$^-$, so 1 mol of KCl gives 2 mol of particles and i = 2.\nFormula: i = $\\frac{\\text{normal molar mass}}{\\text{abnormal molar mass}}$.\nSubstituting: abnormal molar mass = 74.5/2.\nSo the experimental molar mass = 37.25 g mol$^{-1}$, which is lower than the true value. 24.83 g mol$^{-1}$ would require 3 particles per formula unit, and 149 g mol$^{-1}$ would correspond to association.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":125,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A car antifreeze is a 35% (V/V) solution of ethylene glycol in water. The density of pure ethylene glycol is 1.11 g mL$^{-1}$ and the density of the antifreeze solution is 1.05 g mL$^{-1}$. What is the mass percentage of ethylene glycol in the antifreeze?","image":"","options":["35.0%","33.3%","38.9%","37.0%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: 100 mL of solution contains 35 mL of ethylene glycol.\nMass of glycol = 35 × 1.11 = 38.85 g.\nMass of solution = 100 × 1.05 = 105 g.\nFormula: mass % = (mass of glycol/mass of solution) × 100 = 38.85/105 × 100 = 37.0%.\nThe value 38.9% comes from wrongly taking the solution mass as 100 g. The value 33.3% comes from using 35 g of glycol instead of 38.85 g.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":126,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"An aqueous solution of urea is labelled 0.5 m. Which of the following correctly describes this solution?","image":"","options":["0.5 mol of urea is dissolved in enough water to make 1 L of solution","0.5 mol of urea is dissolved in 1 kg of water","0.5 mol of urea is present in 1 kg of solution","0.5 g of urea is dissolved in 1 kg of water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Molality (m) is the number of moles of solute per kilogram of solvent, not per kilogram of solution.\nSo a 0.5 m urea solution has 0.5 mol of urea dissolved in 1 kg of water.\nOption (1) describes 0.5 M (molarity), which uses litres of solution.\nOption (3) wrongly uses the mass of the solution, and option (4) uses grams instead of moles.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":127,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Commercial bleaching solution contains 3.62% sodium hypochlorite (NaOCl) by mass in water. What mass of NaOCl is present in 250 g of this bleaching solution?","image":"","options":["9.05 g","3.62 g","9.39 g","6.90 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: mass % of NaOCl = 3.62%, mass of solution = 250 g.\nFormula: mass % = (mass of component / total mass of solution) × 100, so mass of component = (mass % × mass of solution)/100.\nSubstituting: mass of NaOCl = 3.62 × 250 / 100 = 9.05 g.\nThe trap is using the mass of water as the base. That gives 9.39 g, but mass % is always taken relative to the total mass of the solution.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":128,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"2.4 g of a non-volatile non-electrolyte is dissolved in 75 g of benzene. The freezing point of benzene is lowered by 0.512 K. $K_f$ for benzene = 5.12 K kg $mol^{-1}$. What is the molar mass of the solute?","image":"","options":["320 g $mol^{-1}$","160 g $mol^{-1}$","640 g $mol^{-1}$","32 g $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $w_2$ = 2.4 g, $w_1$ = 75 g, $\\Delta T_f$ = 0.512 K, $K_f$ = 5.12 K kg $mol^{-1}$.\nFormula: $M_2 = \\frac{K_f \\times w_2 \\times 1000}{\\Delta T_f \\times w_1}$.\nSubstituting: $M_2$ = (5.12 × 2.4 × 1000)/(0.512 × 75) = 12288/38.4.\nSo $M_2$ = 320 g $mol^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":129,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Components 1 and 2 form an ideal solution, with $p_1^0 < p_2^0$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The total vapour pressure varies linearly with $x_2$.\n(ii) For any composition with $0 < x_2 < 1$, the mole fraction of 2 in the vapour exceeds its mole fraction in the liquid.\n(iii) $\\Delta_{mix}H = 0$ and $\\Delta_{mix}V = 0$.\n(iv) The total vapour pressure increases as $x_1$ increases.\n(v) Raoult's law is a special case of Henry's law in which $K_H$ equals the vapour pressure of the pure component.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $p_{total} = p_1^0 + (p_2^0 - p_1^0)x_2$, which is linear in $x_2$.\n(ii) Correct: $y_2 = x_2p_2^0/p_{total}$, and $p_{total} < p_2^0$, so $y_2 > x_2$. The vapour is richer in the more volatile component.\n(iii) Correct: these are defining features of an ideal solution.\n(iv) Incorrect: increasing $x_1$ adds more of the less volatile component, so $p_{total}$ falls.\n(v) Correct: when $K_H = p^0$, Henry's law reduces to Raoult's law.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":130,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"For an ideal solution of two volatile liquids, the total vapour pressure is $p_{total} = p_1^0 + (p_2^0 - p_1^0)x_2$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The total vapour pressure can be related to the mole fraction of any one component.\n(ii) A plot of $p_{total}$ versus $x_2$ is a straight line of slope $(p_2^0 - p_1^0)$.\n(iii) The total vapour pressure always increases as the mole fraction of component 1 increases.\n(iv) If $p_1^0 = p_2^0$, the total vapour pressure is independent of composition.\n(v) Each partial vapour pressure line passes through zero at the composition where that component is absent.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: substituting $x_1 = 1 - x_2$ gives $p_{total}$ in terms of a single mole fraction.\n(ii) Correct: the equation is linear in $x_2$ with slope $p_2^0 - p_1^0$.\n(iii) Incorrect: $p_{total}$ rises or falls with $x_1$ depending on whether $p_1^0$ is greater or smaller than $p_2^0$.\n(iv) Correct: the slope becomes zero.\n(v) Correct: $p_i = p_i^0 x_i$ is zero when $x_i = 0$.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":131,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Liquids A and B form an ideal solution at a certain temperature. The vapour pressures of pure A and pure B are 300 mm Hg and 600 mm Hg respectively. The mole fraction of A in the liquid is 0.6. What is the mole fraction of B in the vapour in equilibrium with this liquid?","image":"","options":["0.40","0.571","0.429","0.60"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $p_A^0$ = 300 mm Hg, $p_B^0$ = 600 mm Hg, $x_A$ = 0.6, so $x_B$ = 0.4.\nFormula: $p_i = x_i p_i^0$ (Raoult's law) and $y_B = \\frac{p_B}{p_{total}}$ (Dalton's law).\nSubstituting: $p_A$ = 180 mm Hg, $p_B$ = 240 mm Hg, so $p_{total}$ = 420 mm Hg.\nSo $y_B$ = 240/420 = 0.571. The vapour is richer in B, the more volatile liquid.\nNote: 0.40 is $x_B$ (liquid phase) and 0.429 is $y_A$, so both are traps.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":132,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following semipermeable membranes is synthetic rather than of animal or vegetable origin?","image":"","options":["Pig's bladder","Parchment","Cellophane","Plant cell membrane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that semipermeable membranes can occur naturally, like pig's bladder (animal origin) or parchment, or can be synthetic.\nCellophane is the synthetic example given.\nPig's bladder and plant cell membranes are clearly natural.\nParchment is the tempting distractor, but NCERT lists it with the naturally occurring membranes, not the synthetic ones.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":133,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Dissolving 1.0 mol of sucrose in 1 kg of water lowers the vapour pressure of water by nearly the same amount as dissolving 1.0 mol of urea in 1 kg of water at the same temperature.\nReason (R): The lowering of vapour pressure by a non-volatile, non-electrolyte solute depends on the number of solute particles and not on their nature.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: both solutions contain 1 mol of solute particles in the same amount of water, so the mole fraction of solute is the same.\nR is true: vapour pressure lowering is a colligative property.\nR directly explains A, because sucrose and urea differ in nature but give equal numbers of particles.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":134,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"What is the SI-based unit of the ebullioscopic constant $K_b$?","image":"","options":["K kg $mol^{-1}$","K mol $kg^{-1}$","kg $mol^{-1}$","$K^{-1}$ kg $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The relation is $\\Delta T_b = K_b m$, so $K_b = \\frac{\\Delta T_b}{m}$.\nThe unit of $\\Delta T_b$ is K and the unit of molality is mol $kg^{-1}$.\nDividing gives K ÷ (mol $kg^{-1}$) = K kg $mol^{-1}$.\nOption (2) inverts the molality unit, and option (3) drops the kelvin.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":135,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a solution showing positive deviation from Raoult's law, both $\\Delta_{mix}H$ and $\\Delta_{mix}V$ are positive.\nStatement II: Solutions showing a large positive deviation from Raoult's law form maximum boiling azeotropes.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In positive deviation, A-B attractions are weaker than A-A and B-B attractions. Energy must be absorbed to mix the liquids ($\\Delta_{mix}H > 0$), and the molecules are held less tightly, so the volume expands ($\\Delta_{mix}V > 0$).\nStatement II is incorrect. A large positive deviation gives a minimum boiling azeotrope, such as ethanol-water.\nMaximum boiling azeotropes come from a large negative deviation, such as nitric acid-water.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":136,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Liquids A and B form an ideal solution at 350 K. The vapour pressures of pure A and pure B are 120 kPa and 40 kPa respectively. If the total vapour pressure over the solution is 70 kPa, what is the mole fraction of A in the liquid?","image":"","options":["0.375","0.625","0.583","0.500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p_A^0$ = 120 kPa, $p_B^0$ = 40 kPa, $p_{total}$ = 70 kPa\nFormula: $p_{total} = p_B^0 + (p_A^0 - p_B^0)x_A$\nSubstituting: 70 = 40 + 80$x_A$, so $x_A$ = 30/80\nSo $x_A$ = 0.375. The value 0.625 is $x_B$, a common mix-up.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":137,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"1.26 g of oxalic acid dihydrate ($H_2C_2O_4\\cdot2H_2O$, molar mass 126 g mol$^{-1}$) is dissolved in water. What volume of 0.20 M NaOH is required to neutralise it completely?\n$H_2C_2O_4 + 2NaOH \\rightarrow Na_2C_2O_4 + 2H_2O$","image":"","options":["50 mL","100 mL","200 mL","25 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: mass of oxalic acid dihydrate = 1.26 g, M = 126 g mol$^{-1}$, so moles of acid = 0.010 mol.\nFrom the equation, 1 mol of acid reacts with 2 mol of NaOH, so moles of NaOH = 0.020 mol.\nFormula: $V = \\frac{\\text{moles}}{M} = \\frac{0.020}{0.20}$ L = 0.100 L.\nSo V = 100 mL. Option (1), 50 mL, ignores the 1 : 2 stoichiometry.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":138,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following binary solutions is the solute a liquid and the solvent a solid?","image":"","options":["Solution of hydrogen in palladium","Amalgam of mercury with sodium","Camphor in nitrogen gas","Copper dissolved in gold"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In an amalgam of mercury with sodium, liquid mercury is the solute and solid sodium is the solvent.\nHydrogen in palladium is a gas in a solid.\nCamphor in nitrogen is a solid in a gas.\nCopper in gold is a solid in a solid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":139,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"An antifreeze solution is to be made by dissolving ethylene glycol ($C_2H_6O_2$) in 2.0 kg of water so that the freezing point of the solution is lowered by 9.3 K. $K_f$ for water = 1.86 K kg $mol^{-1}$. Atomic masses: C = 12, H = 1, O = 16. What mass of ethylene glycol is required?","image":"","options":["620 g","310 g","1240 g","155 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta T_f$ = 9.3 K, $K_f$ = 1.86 K kg $mol^{-1}$, mass of water = 2.0 kg, M($C_2H_6O_2$) = 62 g $mol^{-1}$.\nFormula: $m = \\frac{\\Delta T_f}{K_f}$.\nSubstituting: m = 9.3/1.86 = 5 mol $kg^{-1}$, so moles of glycol = 5 × 2.0 = 10 mol.\nSo mass = 10 × 62 = 620 g.\nOption (2) forgets to multiply by the 2 kg of water.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":140,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Henry's law constants ($K_H$) in water at 298 K are: argon 40.3 kbar, CO$_2$ 1.67 kbar, methane 0.413 kbar and vinyl chloride 0.611 kbar. At the same partial pressure and temperature, which gas has the highest solubility (mole fraction) in water?","image":"","options":["Argon","CO$_2$","Methane","Vinyl chloride"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Henry's law: $p = K_H x$, so $x = \\frac{p}{K_H}$.\nAt a fixed partial pressure, the gas with the smallest $K_H$ has the largest mole fraction in solution.\nMethane has the lowest $K_H$ (0.413 kbar), so it is the most soluble of these four gases.\nArgon has the largest $K_H$, so it is the least soluble. Choosing argon would mean reading the relation the wrong way round.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":141,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Water at 293 K is in equilibrium with air at a total pressure of 1.00 bar. The air contains 21% $O_2$ and 79% $N_2$ by volume. The Henry's law constants at 293 K are $K_H(O_2)$ = 34.86 kbar and $K_H(N_2)$ = 76.48 kbar. What is the ratio of moles of $O_2$ to moles of $N_2$ dissolved in the water?","image":"","options":["0.583","0.266","0.121","1.71"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p_{O_2} = 0.21$ bar and $p_{N_2} = 0.79$ bar, from Dalton's law.\nFormula: $x = \\frac{p}{K_H}$, so $\\frac{n_{O_2}}{n_{N_2}} = \\frac{p_{O_2}/K_{H,O_2}}{p_{N_2}/K_{H,N_2}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{0.21/34.86}{0.79/76.48} = \\frac{6.02 \\times 10^{-3}}{1.033 \\times 10^{-2}} = 0.583$.\nSo the ratio = 0.583. The value 0.266 ignores the different $K_H$ values, and 0.121 uses them inverted.\nThe dissolved gas is therefore richer in $O_2$ than air itself.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":142,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Liquids A and B form an ideal solution. At a certain temperature, the vapour pressures of pure A and pure B are 300 mm Hg and 500 mm Hg respectively. What is the total vapour pressure over a solution in which the mole fraction of A is 0.4?","image":"","options":["380 mm Hg","400 mm Hg","420 mm Hg","460 mm Hg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $p_A^0$ = 300 mm Hg, $p_B^0$ = 500 mm Hg, $x_A$ = 0.4, so $x_B$ = 0.6.\nFormula: $p_{total} = x_A p_A^0 + x_B p_B^0$ (Raoult's law combined with Dalton's law).\nSubstituting: $p_{total}$ = 0.4 × 300 + 0.6 × 500 = 120 + 300 mm Hg.\nSo $p_{total}$ = 420 mm Hg.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":143,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Ethanol obtained by fermentation cannot be concentrated beyond about 95% (by volume) by fractional distillation.\nReason (R): At the azeotropic composition, the liquid and its vapour have the same composition.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: ethanol-water forms a minimum boiling azeotrope at about 95% ethanol by volume.\nR is true: an azeotrope is a constant-boiling mixture whose liquid and vapour have the same composition.\nFractional distillation works only because the vapour has a different composition from the liquid. Once the compositions become equal, no further enrichment is possible.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":144,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The molar mass of KCl determined from the depression in freezing point of its aqueous solution is higher than its true molar mass.\nReason (R): In water, KCl dissociates into $K^+$ and $Cl^-$ ions, which increases the number of solute particles.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true. One KCl unit gives two ions, so the number of particles increases (i is close to 2).\nMore particles give a larger observed $\\Delta T_f$.\nSince $M_2 = \\frac{K_f w_2 1000}{\\Delta T_f w_1}$, a larger $\\Delta T_f$ gives a smaller calculated molar mass, close to 37.25 g/mol instead of 74.5 g/mol.\nSo A is false: dissociation gives a lower abnormal molar mass, not a higher one.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":145,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"The van't Hoff factors observed for 0.01 m aqueous solutions are: NaCl = 1.94, $MgSO_4$ = 1.53 and $K_2SO_4$ = 2.70. Glucose does not dissociate. What is the correct order of increasing depression in freezing point for 0.01 m aqueous solutions of these four solutes?","image":"","options":["Glucose < NaCl < $MgSO_4$ < $K_2SO_4$","Glucose < $MgSO_4$ < NaCl < $K_2SO_4$","$K_2SO_4$ < NaCl < $MgSO_4$ < glucose","Glucose < $MgSO_4$ = NaCl < $K_2SO_4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At equal molality, $\\Delta T_f = iK_f m$, so the depression is proportional to i.\nThe i values are glucose 1, $MgSO_4$ 1.53, NaCl 1.94, $K_2SO_4$ 2.70.\nThe increasing order of $\\Delta T_f$ is therefore glucose < $MgSO_4$ < NaCl < $K_2SO_4$.\nOption (4) assumes complete dissociation (i = 2 for both NaCl and $MgSO_4$), which ignores the observed values.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":146,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"An aqueous solution of urea ($NH_2CONH_2$, molar mass 60 g mol$^{-1}$) is 2.0 molal. Its density is 1.12 g mL$^{-1}$. Take the molar mass of water as 18 g mol$^{-1}$.\nConsider the following statements:\n(I) The mole fraction of urea in the solution is about 0.035.\n(II) The molarity of the solution is 2.0 mol L$^{-1}$.\n(III) The mass percentage of urea in the solution is 12.0%.\n(IV) The solution contains about 27.8 mol of water per mole of urea.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Take 1000 g of water: it contains 2.0 mol (120 g) urea and $\\frac{1000}{18} = 55.56$ mol water.\n(I) Correct: $x_{urea} = \\frac{2}{2 + 55.56} = 0.035$.\n(II) Correct: mass of solution = 1120 g, so volume = $\\frac{1120}{1.12} = 1000$ mL. Molarity = 2.0 mol in 1.0 L = 2.0 M.\n(III) Incorrect: mass % = $\\frac{120}{1120} \\times 100 = 10.7\\%$. The value 12% comes from dividing by the mass of water instead of the mass of solution.\n(IV) Correct: $\\frac{55.56}{2} = 27.8$ mol of water per mole of urea.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":147,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Once a saturated solution of a solid is formed, dissolution of the solid stops completely.\nStatement II: In a saturated solution, the concentration of solute remains constant at the given temperature and pressure.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. At saturation, dissolution and crystallisation both continue at the same rate. The equilibrium is dynamic, not stopped.\nStatement II is correct. Because the two rates are equal, the number of particles entering the solution equals the number leaving it. The concentration therefore stays constant and equals the solubility.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":148,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal binary solution of components 1 and 2 (component 1 less volatile, $p_1^0 < p_2^0$) is plotted at constant temperature. The x-axis is the mole fraction $x_2$ and the y-axis is vapour pressure. The total vapour pressure appears as a straight line (line III). At $x_2 = 0$, line III meets the vapour pressure axis at:","image":"","options":["zero","$p_2^0$","$p_1^0$","$\\frac{p_1^0 + p_2^0}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The total pressure is $p_{total} = p_1^0 + (p_2^0 - p_1^0)x_2$.\nAt $x_2 = 0$ only component 1 is present, so $p_{total} = p_1^0$. This is the minimum value of line III.\nZero is the value of the partial-pressure line of component 2 at $x_2 = 0$, not of the total-pressure line.\n$p_2^0$ is the maximum value, reached at $x_2 = 1$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":149,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Benzene (molar mass 78 g mol$^{-1}$) freezes at 278.6 K, and its enthalpy of fusion is 9.87 kJ mol$^{-1}$. Using $K_f = \\frac{R M_1 T_f^2}{1000\\,\\Delta_{fus}H}$ with R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, find the depression in freezing point when 1.28 g of naphthalene (molar mass 128 g mol$^{-1}$) is dissolved in 50 g of benzene.","image":"","options":["1.02 K","0.51 K","2.04 K","0.26 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $M_1$ = 78 g mol$^{-1}$, $T_f$ = 278.6 K, $\\Delta_{fus}H$ = 9870 J mol$^{-1}$, R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nStep 1: $K_f = \\frac{8.314 \\times 78 \\times (278.6)^2}{1000 \\times 9870}$, which gives about 5.10 K kg mol$^{-1}$.\nStep 2: moles of naphthalene = 1.28/128 = 0.01 mol, so $m$ = 0.01/0.050 = 0.2 mol kg$^{-1}$.\nStep 3: $\\Delta T_f = K_f m$ = 5.10 × 0.2 = 1.02 K.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":150,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"For the solubility of HCl gas in cyclohexane at 293 K, the partial pressure of HCl (y-axis) is plotted against the mole fraction of HCl in solution (x-axis). The plot is a straight line passing through the origin. What does the slope of this line represent?","image":"","options":["The vapour pressure of pure cyclohexane","The reciprocal of the Henry's law constant","The Henry's law constant, $K_H$","The mole fraction of HCl in the vapour phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Henry's law is $p = K_H x$. This has the form $y = mx$ with zero intercept.\nWhen $p$ is plotted against $x$, the slope is therefore $K_H$.\nThe slope would equal $\\frac{1}{K_H}$ only if $x$ were plotted against $p$, so option (2) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":151,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Ethanol + acetone\n(B) Chloroform + acetone\n(C) Benzene + toluene\n(D) Nitric acid + water (68% acid by mass)\nList-II:\n(P) Nearly ideal solution\n(Q) Positive deviation because added molecules break existing hydrogen bonds\n(R) Negative deviation because the H of one component hydrogen bonds with the C=O of the other\n(S) Azeotrope boiling at 393.5 K","image":"","options":["A-Q, B-R, C-P, D-S","A-R, B-Q, C-P, D-S","A-Q, B-R, C-S, D-P","A-P, B-R, C-Q, D-S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Acetone molecules break some hydrogen bonds of ethanol, giving positive deviation: Q.\n(B) Chloroform's H forms a hydrogen bond with the carbonyl O of acetone, giving negative deviation: R.\n(C) Benzene and toluene have similar interactions, so the solution is nearly ideal: P.\n(D) The 68% nitric acid–water mixture is a maximum boiling azeotrope at 393.5 K: S.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":152,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Sea water is approximated as a 0.60 M NaCl solution (assume complete dissociation, i = 2). For desalination by reverse osmosis at 300 K, what pressure must the applied pressure on the sea-water side exceed? (R = 0.083 L bar mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["29.9 bar","14.9 bar","59.8 bar","2.49 bar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: C = 0.60 M, i = 2, T = 300 K, R = 0.083 L bar mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: reverse osmosis needs a pressure greater than the osmotic pressure, $\\Pi = iCRT$.\nSubstituting: $\\Pi = 2 \\times 0.60 \\times 0.083 \\times 300$\nSo $\\Pi$ = 29.9 bar, and the applied pressure must exceed about 29.9 bar for pure water to flow out of the sea water.\nTaking i = 1 would wrongly give 14.9 bar.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":153,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Concentration in ppm can be expressed as mass to mass, volume to volume or mass to volume.\n(ii) A 10% (w/w) glucose solution contains 10 g of glucose dissolved in 100 g of water.\n(iii) Mass by volume percentage is the mass of solute dissolved in 100 mL of solution.\n(iv) Mass percentage of a solution does not change with temperature.\n(v) A 35% (V/V) aqueous ethylene glycol solution lowers the freezing point of water to about 255.4 K.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. Like percentage, ppm can be expressed as w/w, v/v or w/V.\n(ii) Incorrect. It means 10 g glucose in 90 g water, giving 100 g of solution.\n(iii) Correct. This is the definition of w/V %.\n(iv) Correct. Masses do not depend on temperature.\n(v) Correct. NCERT gives 255.4 K (–17.6 °C) for 35% antifreeze.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":154,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Dissolution\n(b) Crystallisation\n(c) Saturated solution\n(d) Unsaturated solution\nList-II\n(i) Solute particles in solution collide with the solid and separate out of solution\n(ii) Solid solute particles go into the solution and its concentration increases\n(iii) More solute can still be dissolved at the same temperature\n(iv) In dynamic equilibrium with undissolved solute","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Dissolution: solid particles enter the solution, so the concentration rises → (ii).\n(b) Crystallisation: dissolved particles collide with the solid and separate out → (i).\n(c) Saturated solution: in dynamic equilibrium with undissolved solute → (iv).\n(d) Unsaturated solution: can still dissolve more solute at the same temperature → (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":155,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"For an ideal binary solution of two volatile liquids 1 and 2 at constant temperature, with $p_1^0 < p_2^0$, vapour pressure is plotted against mole fraction. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The plot of $p_{total}$ against $x_2$ is a straight line.","The partial pressure lines of components 1 and 2 pass through the points where $x_1 = 1$ and $x_2 = 1$ respectively.","The total vapour pressure has its minimum value $p_1^0$ and its maximum value $p_2^0$.","The vapour in equilibrium with the solution is richer in component 1 than the liquid is."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $p_{total} = p_1^0 + (p_2^0 - p_1^0)x_2$ is linear in $x_2$.\n(2) Correct: each partial pressure line reaches the pure-component vapour pressure at a mole fraction of 1.\n(3) Correct: $p_{total}$ ranges from $p_1^0$ (pure 1) to $p_2^0$ (pure 2).\n(4) Incorrect: the vapour is always richer in the more volatile component. Since $p_2^0 > p_1^0$, that component is 2, not 1.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":156,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A 0.10 m aqueous solution of a weak monobasic acid HA freezes at a temperature 0.2046 K below the freezing point of pure water. Take $K_f$ (water) = 1.86 K kg mol$^{-1}$, assume molality ≈ molarity, and assume complete solubility. What is the dissociation constant $K_a$ of HA?","image":"","options":["$1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ mol L$^{-1}$","$1.23 \\times 10^{-4}$ mol L$^{-1}$","$1.11 \\times 10^{-3}$ mol L$^{-1}$","$1.11 \\times 10^{-2}$ mol L$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.10, observed $\\Delta T_f$ = 0.2046 K, $K_f$ = 1.86 K kg mol$^{-1}$.\nCalculated $\\Delta T_f$ = 1.86 × 0.10 = 0.186 K, so i = 0.2046/0.186 = 1.10.\nFor HA ⇌ H$^+$ + A$^-$, i = 1 + α, so α = 0.10.\nFormula: $K_a = \\frac{c\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha}$.\nSubstituting: $K_a$ = 0.10 × (0.10)$^2$/0.90 = $1.11 \\times 10^{-3}$ mol L$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":157,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"At 293 K, the Henry's law constants $K_H$ (in kbar) for some gases in water are: He = 144.97, $H_2$ = 69.16, $N_2$ = 76.48 and $O_2$ = 34.86. Which of these gases is the least soluble in water at 293 K when each is at the same partial pressure?","image":"","options":["Helium","Hydrogen","Nitrogen","Oxygen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Henry's law is $p = K_H x$, so $x = \\frac{p}{K_H}$.\nAt the same partial pressure, a larger $K_H$ gives a smaller mole fraction of dissolved gas, which means lower solubility.\nHelium has the largest $K_H$ (144.97 kbar), so it is the least soluble.\nOxygen has the smallest $K_H$, so it is the most soluble of the four. Choosing it confuses a large $K_H$ with high solubility.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":158,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Liquids A and B form an ideal solution. At a certain temperature the vapour pressures of pure A and pure B are 300 mm Hg and 100 mm Hg respectively. The vapour in equilibrium with a particular liquid mixture contains equal numbers of moles of A and B. What is the mole fraction of A in the liquid?","image":"","options":["0.25","0.50","0.75","0.33"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $p_A^0$ = 300 mm Hg, $p_B^0$ = 100 mm Hg, and $y_A = y_B$.\nFormula: $p_i = y_i p_{total}$. Equal $y$ values mean equal partial pressures, so $x_A p_A^0 = x_B p_B^0$.\nSubstituting: $300\\,x_A = 100(1 - x_A)$, which gives $400\\,x_A = 100$.\nSo $x_A$ = 0.25. The liquid is poorer in the more volatile component A, while the vapour is richer in it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":159,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Raoult's law can be treated as a special case of Henry's law in which the Henry's law constant $K_H$ becomes equal to $p_1^0$, the vapour pressure of the pure component.\nStatement II: For a gas at a given partial pressure, the higher the value of $K_H$, the higher is its solubility in the liquid.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Both laws have the form p ∝ x. Henry's law is $p = K_H x$ and Raoult's law is $p_1 = p_1^0 x_1$, so the two match when $K_H = p_1^0$.\nStatement II is incorrect. From $x = p/K_H$, a larger $K_H$ at the same pressure gives a smaller mole fraction, so the gas is less soluble.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":160,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"In an aqueous NaOH solution, the mole fraction of NaOH is 0.10 and the density of the solution is 1.2 g mL$^{-1}$. What is the molarity of the solution? (Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1)","image":"","options":["5.94 M","6.17 M","5.00 M","7.13 M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Take 1 mol of total particles: 0.1 mol NaOH (0.1 × 40 = 4.0 g) and 0.9 mol water (0.9 × 18 = 16.2 g).\nMass of solution = 20.2 g, so volume = 20.2/1.2 = 16.83 mL = 0.01683 L\nMolarity = 0.1/0.01683 = 5.94 M\nThe value 6.17 is the molality, 0.1 mol per 0.0162 kg of water.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":161,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (concentration unit) with List-II (typical use mentioned in NCERT).\nList-I:\n(a) Mass percentage (w/w)\n(b) Volume percentage (V/V)\n(c) Mass by volume percentage (w/V)\n(d) Parts per million\nList-II:\n(i) Concentration of pollutants in water or atmosphere\n(ii) Medicine and pharmacy\n(iii) Industrial chemical applications, e.g. bleaching solution\n(iv) Solutions of liquids, e.g. car antifreeze","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Mass % is common in industrial chemistry, for example 3.62% NaOCl bleach, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) Volume % is used for liquid solutions, for example 35% (v/v) ethylene glycol antifreeze, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) Mass by volume % is common in medicine and pharmacy, so (c)-(ii).\n(d) ppm is used for trace amounts such as pollutants, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":162,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Aquatic species are more comfortable in cold water than in warm water.\nReason (R): The Henry's law constant $K_H$ of oxygen in water increases with rise in temperature, so its solubility decreases.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that aquatic life prefers cold water.\nR is true. $K_H$ for $O_2$ is 34.86 kbar at 293 K and 46.82 kbar at 303 K. Since $p = K_H x$, a larger $K_H$ means a smaller mole fraction of dissolved gas.\nSo cold water holds more dissolved oxygen, and R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":163,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) For water, the cryoscopic constant is numerically larger than the ebullioscopic constant.\n(ii) Among water, benzene, cyclohexane and carbon tetrachloride, carbon tetrachloride has the largest $K_f$.\n(iii) For the same molality of a non-electrolyte, the depression in freezing point is greater in benzene than in water.\n(iv) A 35% (v/v) aqueous solution of ethylene glycol used as antifreeze freezes at about 255.4 K.\n(v) Depression in freezing point of a dilute solution is directly proportional to its molarity.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: for water, $K_f$ = 1.86 and $K_b$ = 0.52 K kg mol$^{-1}$.\n(ii) Correct: $K_f$ of CCl$_4$ is 31.8, which exceeds cyclohexane (20.0), benzene (5.12) and water (1.86).\n(iii) Correct: since 5.12 > 1.86, $\\Delta T_f = K_f m$ is larger in benzene.\n(iv) Correct: this is the NCERT value (–17.6 °C).\n(v) Incorrect: $\\Delta T_f$ is proportional to molality, not molarity.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":164,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (observation/practice) with List-II (explanation based on gas solubility):\nList-I\n(a) Soft drink bottles are sealed under high pressure\n(b) Scuba tanks contain air diluted with helium\n(c) Aquatic species are more comfortable in cold water\n(d) Climbers feel weak at high altitudes\nList-II\n(i) Lower partial pressure of $O_2$ lowers dissolved $O_2$ in blood\n(ii) Increases the solubility of $CO_2$\n(iii) $K_H$ of $O_2$ is lower at lower temperature, so more $O_2$ dissolves\n(iv) Reduces the bends and the toxic effects of high dissolved nitrogen","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(ii): High $CO_2$ pressure raises the solubility of $CO_2$ in the drink, by Henry's law.\n(b)-(iv): Helium dilution helps avoid the bends and the toxic effects of high nitrogen concentration in the blood.\n(c)-(iii): $K_H$ for $O_2$ is smaller at lower temperature, so cold water holds more dissolved oxygen.\n(d)-(i): Low partial pressure of $O_2$ at altitude leads to low blood oxygen, which is anoxia.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":165,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A solution is a homogeneous mixture whose composition and properties are uniform throughout","In a gaseous solution, the solute may be a solid, a liquid or a gas","Oxygen dissolved in water and a mixture of oxygen and nitrogen gases are both gaseous solutions because the solute is a gas in each case","A binary solution contains two components, each of which may be solid, liquid or gas"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: this is NCERT's definition of a homogeneous mixture.\n(2) Correct: camphor in N2 (solid), chloroform in N2 (liquid) and O2 in N2 (gas) are all gaseous solutions.\n(3) Incorrect: the solution type depends on the solvent, not the solute. O2 in water is a liquid solution, while O2 + N2 is a gaseous solution.\n(4) Correct: this is how NCERT describes binary solutions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":166,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following ways of expressing the concentration of an aqueous solution changes with a change in temperature?","image":"","options":["Mass percentage (w/w)","Mole fraction","Mass by volume percentage (w/V)","Parts per million by mass"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Concentration terms based only on masses or moles do not change with temperature, because mass does not change on heating.\nMass %, mole fraction and ppm (w/w) are therefore temperature independent.\nMass by volume % uses the volume of the solution, which expands or contracts with temperature. This is the same reason molarity depends on temperature.\nSo the w/V value changes when the temperature changes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":167,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A student finds the molar mass of KCl by measuring the elevation in boiling point of its dilute aqueous solution. She assumes KCl is completely dissociated and ignores interionic attractions. What experimental (abnormal) molar mass will she obtain? (Atomic masses: K = 39, Cl = 35.5)","image":"","options":["37.25 g mol$^{-1}$","74.5 g mol$^{-1}$","149 g mol$^{-1}$","24.83 g mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: normal molar mass of KCl = 39 + 35.5 = 74.5 g mol$^{-1}$. Complete dissociation gives K$^+$ + Cl$^-$, so i = 2.\nFormula: $i = \\frac{\\text{Normal molar mass}}{\\text{Abnormal molar mass}}$\nSubstituting: abnormal molar mass = 74.5/2.\nSo the experimental molar mass = 37.25 g mol$^{-1}$, lower than the true value, as expected for dissociation.\n149 g mol$^{-1}$ would apply to association, not dissociation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":168,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The osmotic pressure method is particularly useful for determining the molar masses of proteins and polymers.\nReason (R): In the osmotic pressure method, the molality of the solution is used instead of its molarity.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Osmotic pressure is measured around room temperature, which suits biomolecules that are unstable at higher temperatures. Its magnitude is also large even for very dilute solutions, which helps with polymers of poor solubility.\nR is false. The equation $\\Pi = CRT$ uses molarity (C), not molality. NCERT lists the use of molarity instead of molality as an advantage of this method.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":169,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"How many grams of a 40% (w/w) aqueous solution of a solute must be mixed with 150 g of a 10% (w/w) solution of the same solute so that the resulting solution is 20% (w/w)?","image":"","options":["50 g","100 g","75 g","150 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 150 g of 10% solution, x g of 40% solution, target 20%.\nFormula: total solute = 0.20 × total solution mass.\nSubstituting: 0.10(150) + 0.40x = 0.20(150 + x).\nThis gives 15 + 0.40x = 30 + 0.20x, so 0.20x = 15 and x = 75 g.\nSo 75 g of the 40% solution is needed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":170,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Equal amounts (in moles) of acetic acid and trifluoroacetic acid are dissolved separately in equal masses of water. The solution of trifluoroacetic acid shows the larger depression in freezing point.\nStatement II: The strongly electron-withdrawing fluorine atoms make trifluoroacetic acid a stronger acid. It therefore dissociates to a greater extent and has a larger van't Hoff factor.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The observed order of $\\Delta T_f$ for equal amounts is acetic < trichloroacetic < trifluoroacetic acid.\nStatement II is correct. Fluorine is the most electronegative element. Its electron-withdrawing effect stabilises the carboxylate ion, so the acid ionises more.\nMore ionisation means more particles, a larger i and hence a larger $\\Delta T_f = iK_fm$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":171,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Fish and other aquatic species are more comfortable in cold water than in warm water.\nStatement II: The Henry's law constant $K_H$ of oxygen in water increases as the temperature rises.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II: NCERT Table 1.2 shows $K_H$ of O$_2$ is 34.86 kbar at 293 K and 46.82 kbar at 303 K. It increases, so Statement II is correct.\nSince $p = K_H x$, a higher $K_H$ means a lower mole fraction of dissolved gas at the same pressure. Warm water therefore holds less O$_2$.\nCold water holds more dissolved oxygen, so Statement I is also correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":172,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"A 0.02 mol kg$^{-1}$ aqueous solution of a weak monobasic acid HA freezes 0.04092 K below the freezing point of pure water. Take molality ≈ molarity for this dilute solution. What is the dissociation constant $K_a$ of HA? ($K_f$ for water = 1.86 K kg mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["$2.22 \\times 10^{-4}$ mol L$^{-1}$","$2.00 \\times 10^{-4}$ mol L$^{-1}$","$2.22 \\times 10^{-3}$ mol L$^{-1}$","$1.10 \\times 10^{-2}$ mol L$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: calculated $\\Delta T_f$ = 1.86 × 0.02 = 0.0372 K.\nFormula: i = 0.04092/0.0372 = 1.10. For HA ⇌ H$^+$ + A$^-$, i = 1 + α, so α = 0.10.\nSubstituting: $K_a = \\frac{c\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha}$ = (0.02 × 0.01)/0.90.\nSo $K_a$ ≈ 2.22 × 10$^{-4}$ mol L$^{-1}$. Ignoring the (1 − α) term would give 2.00 × 10$^{-4}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":173,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (binary solution) with List-II (type of solution).\nList-I:\n(A) Hydrogen dissolved in palladium\n(B) Amalgam of mercury with sodium\n(C) Camphor in nitrogen gas\n(D) Chloroform mixed with nitrogen gas\nList-II:\n(I) Liquid in gas\n(II) Gas in solid\n(III) Solid in gas\n(IV) Liquid in solid","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) Hydrogen in palladium: the solute is a gas and the solvent is a solid, so it is gas in solid (II).\n(B) Sodium amalgam: liquid mercury is dissolved in solid sodium, so it is liquid in solid (IV).\n(C) Camphor in nitrogen: a solid solute in a gaseous solvent, so it is solid in gas (III).\n(D) Chloroform with nitrogen: a liquid solute in a gaseous solvent, so it is liquid in gas (I).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":174,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Equimolal solutions of the same non-volatile non-electrolyte are prepared in four solvents. Using the values listed in NCERT Table 1.3, which solution shows the largest depression in freezing point?","image":"","options":["Water","Benzene","Cyclohexane","Carbon tetrachloride"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Since $\\Delta T_f = K_f m$, at the same molality the largest $\\Delta T_f$ belongs to the solvent with the largest $K_f$.\nThe $K_f$ values (K kg $mol^{-1}$) are: water 1.86, benzene 5.12, cyclohexane 20.00, carbon tetrachloride 31.8.\nCarbon tetrachloride has the largest value.\nCyclohexane is tempting because its $K_f$ is also large, but it is smaller than that of $CCl_4$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-SOLUTIONS-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Solutions","qno":175,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Solutions","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In desalination by reverse osmosis, a film of cellulose acetate is used because it is permeable to water but impermeable to the ions and impurities present in sea water.\nStatement II: In reverse osmosis, a pressure smaller than the osmotic pressure is applied on the pure solvent side.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT describes cellulose acetate on a support as a workable membrane that lets water through but blocks ions and impurities.\nStatement II is incorrect. Reverse osmosis requires a pressure larger than the osmotic pressure.\nThis pressure is applied on the solution (sea water) side, so pure water is squeezed out.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 1 · Solutions","difficulty":"easy"}]});
